1
00:00:01,706 --> 00:00:14,360
[nm]: So, recently, when I was talking with people in Samaṇadīpa, I was asking and giving some examples of how to talk to your mind, how to communicate.

2
00:00:15,990 --> 00:00:18,846
[nm]: Well, you were there, so you don't answer the questions.

3
00:00:19,710 --> 00:00:30,200
[nm]: So, basically, if we say, if we remember that the mind is the wild animal that's been contained

4
00:00:30,944 --> 00:00:36,450
[nm]: and now, hopefully, being trained, how do you start talking to it?

5
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:45,710
[nm]: How do you start communicating with it, so that it basically does what you tell it to do, not the other way around?

6
00:00:49,057 --> 00:00:50,560
[nm]: How would you talk to it?

7
00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:00,320
[nm]: Would wishing things,

8
00:01:03,072 --> 00:01:10,104
[nm]: make it... make it go along your wishes, like you wish your mind to not be upset.

9
00:01:10,850 --> 00:01:12,073
[nm]: Would that make it not upset?

10
00:01:13,190 --> 00:01:14,980
[nm]: So, you can't talk to it directly, basically.

11
00:01:15,666 --> 00:01:17,620
[nm]: In other words, it doesn't understand you.

12
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:22,513
[nm]: So, how would you be conveying the messages you want to convey?

13
00:01:23,210 --> 00:01:24,393
[nm]: Give me some examples.

14
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:32,460
[sd]: Which kind of, like, get into, so to speak.

15
00:01:32,913 --> 00:01:33,300
[sd]: Sorry?

16
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:36,033
[sd]: [?Would you let it], like, get into, so to speak.

17
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:44,646
[sd]: So, I mean, yeah, the coarsest example is the precepts: you don't let it do that,

18
00:01:45,346 --> 00:01:46,986
[sd]: uhm... these things.

19
00:01:47,093 --> 00:01:53,240
[nm]: So, coarse forms of communication would basically be the precepts already, not letting it.

20
00:01:54,346 --> 00:01:54,660
[sd]: Right.

21
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:59,286
[sd]: But you can kind of carrying that on to, like, just because it wants...

22
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:02,613
[sd]: I was thinking about this the other day, I am not...

23
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:17,824
[sd]: so sure how to put it, but... like the fact that there is something present, like a thought, kind of a pressure to think about something, for example...

24
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:33,170
[sd]: That thought is there, but there is the kind of extra step of the mind being able to, like, like you need to basically give it permission to start.

25
00:02:34,148 --> 00:02:35,948
[sd]: Thinking about these things.

26
00:02:39,032 --> 00:02:41,549
[nm]: Yeah, well, that still kind of comes on the same level, isn't it?

27
00:02:42,010 --> 00:02:42,960
[nm]: It's just restraint.

28
00:02:43,454 --> 00:02:45,156
[nm]: It's not letting it or letting it.

29
00:02:46,030 --> 00:02:51,613
[nm]: So, precepts, sense restraint would be forms of indirect communication.

30
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:55,560
[nm]: So, you are broadly, coarsely, conveying a certain message.

31
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:57,936
[nm]: So, the mind still doesn't know why.

32
00:02:59,388 --> 00:03:01,971
[nm]: The animal hasn't learned for itself.

33
00:03:03,104 --> 00:03:07,779
[nm]: Why it shouldn't be going in those directions, pursuing those pressures.

34
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:10,838
[nm]: It just knows that it can't go there.

35
00:03:13,717 --> 00:03:16,379
[nm]: So, how do you explain to it why?

36
00:03:20,395 --> 00:03:28,122
[nm]: How do you tell a dog... How do you get the dog to prefer to stay rather than to go?

37
00:03:30,570 --> 00:03:31,551
[nm]: Rather than to resist?

38
00:03:34,670 --> 00:03:37,272
[sd]: Well, I mean, you can punish it or you can reward it.

39
00:03:37,730 --> 00:03:42,846
[sd]: I'm just... speaking of a dog, I'm not entirely sure how that would translate, but...

40
00:03:44,726 --> 00:03:45,970
[nm]: Well, no, we're speaking about the mind.

41
00:03:46,297 --> 00:03:46,610
[sd]:  Right.

42
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:47,840
[nm]: I just gave the example.

43
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:50,742
[nm]: It might be more palpable.

44
00:03:53,450 --> 00:03:55,297
[nm]: So, how do you punish the mind? How do you reward it?

45
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:57,948
[nm]: Do you need to punish it? When do you need to punish it?

46
00:04:00,182 --> 00:04:04,085
[sd]: Right, that's... that's where it seems not correct.

47
00:04:08,743 --> 00:04:10,174
[nm]: Well, it's not incorrect.

48
00:04:12,324 --> 00:04:13,491
[nm]: It's the same principle.

49
00:04:15,143 --> 00:04:19,134
[nm]: It is an animal that doesn't speak your language.

50
00:04:20,385 --> 00:04:27,336
[nm]: So, you make it see something, as you said, through a reward.

51
00:04:27,562 --> 00:04:34,070
[nm]: Obviously, you can't just give it a treat, because that would be... well, that would be undoing your training.

52
00:04:35,807 --> 00:04:38,301
[nm]: So, how do you reward your mind?

53
00:04:39,741 --> 00:04:43,632
[nm]: So, it starts to see for itself, so that you are not doing anything...

54
00:04:53,447 --> 00:04:58,618
[nm]: Because the whole point, obviously, the whole point of this, I mean, I think we said it before, but it doesn't matter.

55
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:06,916
[nm]: It's the mind that craves, and you're the one that experiences the results of that pain.

56
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:13,236
[nm]: Well, mind suffers as well, but it's kind of unaware of it, much like an animal would not have enough self-awareness.

57
00:05:14,290 --> 00:05:20,581
[nm]: So, the mind craves, but then you get to pay the price for that, with the suffering that's felt.

58
00:05:21,956 --> 00:05:27,190
[nm]: So, you can say, oh, I don't want to crave, and I'll let go of craving, but you're not the one doing the craving.

59
00:05:28,334 --> 00:05:32,457
[nm]: You're not holding, holding the craving, because if you were, you would let it go instantly.

60
00:05:32,477 --> 00:05:34,399
[nm]: You would not need the Buddha's teaching to tell you that.

61
00:05:35,892 --> 00:05:37,717
[nm]: So, it's just the mind. The mind keeps

62
00:05:38,386 --> 00:05:49,499
[nm]: The wild dog keeps biting the burning embers, because he doesn't know anything better, because he thinks that will destroy them and free it from the pain, and you are the one being burned by that.

63
00:05:51,910 --> 00:05:56,003
[nm]: So, how do you tell that animal? How do you teach it to stop doing it?

64
00:06:00,622 --> 00:06:08,069
[nm]: Obviously, precepts, establishment in virtue, it's not negotiable, because that would be containing the animal.

65
00:06:08,688 --> 00:06:18,942
[nm]: That would be like putting up a fence around, so that at least you know where the animal is, because you're not going to be issuing any commands, right or wrong, if you let the animal loose, if it's gone.

66
00:06:20,911 --> 00:06:36,810
[nm]: Now, obviously, you have contained it, but if that's all you did, well, it's still not tamed, so it's still going to be... Sure, it's probably not going to inflict as much suffering as if somebody who is immoral and not virtuous, but nevertheless, it's still a wild animal.

67
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:47,478
[nm]: There is still plenty of opportunities within the fenced area to crave, to do stuff, and then you would feel pain on account of it.

68
00:06:48,542 --> 00:06:53,318
[nm]: So, you want it to know for itself the craving is bad.

69
00:07:06,368 --> 00:07:22,215
[nm]: That's when the suttas, when it says, like, when a monk becomes an arahant, and his mind is tamed fully, completely, freed from any possibility of craving, it says, oh, it's liberated, and he knows it is liberated.

70
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:24,886
[nm]: He knows that animal is liberated.

71
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:31,106
[nm]: That's why it's like that kind of two-tier knowledge, so to speak.

72
00:07:31,220 --> 00:07:36,984
[nm]: Like, yes, he knows for himself, but he also knows that this thing there, that he's paired with, is liberated.

73
00:07:38,323 --> 00:07:39,798
[nm]: It's unable to crave.

74
00:07:40,890 --> 00:07:57,168
[nm]: So, he cannot have any fear or concern amidst things, even that are unpleasant, even death, like, you know, among death, he doesn't tremble, because he knows the dog is incapable of doing that ever again.

75
00:07:58,914 --> 00:08:02,859
[nm]: And thus, he can never again experience suffering as a result of that.

76
00:08:04,667 --> 00:08:08,532
[nm]: So, it's like the most immediate knowledge.

77
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:14,177
[nm]: So, how do you teach it to stop craving?

78
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:19,006
[nm]: So, let's look at it practically.

79
00:08:19,020 --> 00:08:19,866
[nm]: So, you're restrained.

80
00:08:21,442 --> 00:08:23,226
[nm]: You keep the precepts.

81
00:08:29,080 --> 00:08:34,065
[nm]: You come across, or you come near, the mind, the animal...

82
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:39,851
[nm]: through your moods, through your feelings, through the pressures... What do you do?

83
00:08:39,990 --> 00:08:40,945
[nm]: What do you tell it then?

84
00:08:43,330 --> 00:08:50,130
[nm]: I mean, you could tell it... drop it or what not, but... that might not necessarily work.

85
00:08:51,514 --> 00:08:54,088
[nm]: So, what would be the first thing you would have to do?

86
00:08:57,751 --> 00:08:59,194
[nm]: Say, your mind bothers you.

87
00:09:00,047 --> 00:09:00,907
[nm]: Something's bothering.

88
00:09:01,170 --> 00:09:03,360
[nm]: There's pressure, annoyance, ill will.

89
00:09:06,220 --> 00:09:07,635
[nm]: What message do you want to tell your mind?

90
00:09:08,910 --> 00:09:12,616
[nm]: Yeah, keep digging, keep fighting this: it will go away then, and we'll both will be free.

91
00:09:13,786 --> 00:09:15,130
[nm]: Or, or not? What do you say?

92
00:09:20,035 --> 00:09:23,060
[tn]: You just got to unresist, you know, calm it down.

93
00:09:23,650 --> 00:09:26,302
[tn]: But you, you're not, you're not trying to fight it.

94
00:09:27,610 --> 00:09:28,349
[tn]: You relax.

95
00:09:28,491 --> 00:09:28,970
[nm]: Mm-hmm.

96
00:09:31,072 --> 00:09:31,342
[nm]: Yeah.

97
00:09:31,510 --> 00:09:32,785
[tn]: And when that thing calms down,

98
00:09:36,506 --> 00:09:37,226
[tn]: It's relaxed.

99
00:09:38,450 --> 00:09:41,434
[tn]: And you're finding a relaxing situation.

100
00:09:41,980 --> 00:09:46,746
[md]: But do you need to wait for it to calm down... or is there...?

101
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:48,791
[nm]: Well, you tell me.

102
00:09:50,010 --> 00:09:52,405
[md]: No, I don't think there should... like, you...

103
00:09:52,897 --> 00:10:01,070
[md]: I mean, initially, maybe, yeah, you need to just kind of contain it and say, okay, I'm not gonna... like, it's not allowed... don't allow it to...

104
00:10:02,602 --> 00:10:05,832
[md]: think further in this direction, for example.

105
00:10:05,942 --> 00:10:07,315
[nm]: But is it the animal that's thinking?

106
00:10:08,860 --> 00:10:09,442
[nm]: Or is it you?

107
00:10:10,870 --> 00:10:11,497
[md]: Well, I...

108
00:10:12,117 --> 00:10:16,350
[md]: I'm kind of not used to thinking in terms of this, like, me and the mind.

109
00:10:16,365 --> 00:10:17,795
[md]: That's what we're getting used to.

110
00:10:17,822 --> 00:10:18,450
[md]: Eh, okay.

111
00:10:19,290 --> 00:10:22,116
[nm]: Because obviously... Well, there's a reason for that.

112
00:10:22,454 --> 00:10:27,596
[nm]: So... Because that is the point.

113
00:10:27,597 --> 00:10:31,263
[nm]: I mean, the Buddha did say, the suttas, that you can be

114
00:10:32,138 --> 00:10:41,304
[nm]: like even an outsider can completely experience dispassion towards the body and remove any ownership, but he can't do it towards the mind.

115
00:10:41,801 --> 00:10:42,055
[nm]: Why?

116
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:46,307
[nm]: Because it has been so close to him for so long and he's been appropriating for so long.

117
00:10:46,828 --> 00:10:48,089
[nm]: But it's exactly the same thing.

118
00:10:50,693 --> 00:10:56,110
[nm]: It's a phenomenon in your experience that you just look past and assume yourself to be, but it's not.

119
00:11:02,065 --> 00:11:04,867
[md]: So, the original question was...

120
00:11:08,053 --> 00:11:10,110
[md]: Okay, so, you don't... Do you need to wait?

121
00:11:10,230 --> 00:11:13,900
[nm]: Well, I think, before we now go to the original question, let's go to what is the mind.

122
00:11:16,180 --> 00:11:16,717
[nm]: Here and now.

123
00:11:17,650 --> 00:11:22,814
[nm]: So, I asked... The other question was basically, so, are you doing the thinking?

124
00:11:24,887 --> 00:11:26,580
[nm]: Or is the animal doing the thinking?

125
00:11:26,736 --> 00:11:27,520
[tn]: Who's doing the thinking?

126
00:11:35,180 --> 00:11:37,299
[md]: It really depends what you're referring to by the mind.

127
00:11:37,720 --> 00:11:38,235
[nm]: Citta.

128
00:11:38,962 --> 00:11:40,360
[nm]: I refer to the citta, yeah.

129
00:11:40,608 --> 00:11:41,340
[nm]: That's the animal.

130
00:11:42,909 --> 00:11:44,882
[nm]: You're taming the citta, you're not taming anything else.

131
00:11:45,328 --> 00:11:47,420
[nm]: In the suttas citta is the one liberated, so...

132
00:11:50,526 --> 00:11:51,450
[nm]: So, who's doing the thinking?

133
00:11:51,710 --> 00:11:53,556
[nm]: Where does the thinking come from?

134
00:11:55,210 --> 00:11:56,169
[nm]: From citta or from you?

135
00:11:56,950 --> 00:11:58,375
[sd]: Well, by thinking you don't...

136
00:11:58,520 --> 00:11:59,420
[md]: What are you referring to by you?

137
00:12:00,395 --> 00:12:01,370
[nm]: Everything else that is not citta.

138
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:13,664
[tn]: Yeah, the thoughts would arise, but then you would overthink them, or intend further upon them...

139
00:12:18,163 --> 00:12:25,449
[tn]: because you don't see the first arising, so to speak, of the phenomenon.

140
00:12:27,720 --> 00:12:33,249
[md]: Well, first of all, it's like you allow it to be... you're absorbed with something.

141
00:12:33,366 --> 00:12:34,963
[md]: Like, that's the first thing,

142
00:12:35,249 --> 00:12:45,627
[md]: and then you think further on account of being already not aware of attending to certain things.

143
00:12:45,630 --> 00:12:48,430
[nm]: Okay. Yeah, that's again, say, like, you stop that.

144
00:12:48,640 --> 00:12:49,381
[nm]: You would stop that.

145
00:12:49,820 --> 00:12:52,338
[nm]: But it's now, let's say, you're acutely pressured.

146
00:12:54,504 --> 00:12:56,640
[nm]: Ah, let's give a practical example...

147
00:12:57,350 --> 00:13:02,532
[nm]: Somebody, you know, you're full of ill will... you have thoughts of cruelty towards that person.

148
00:13:04,210 --> 00:13:07,812
[nm]: Are you thinking those thoughts? Or is the mind thinking? Or is citta thinking those thoughts?

149
00:13:07,830 --> 00:13:11,080
[nm]: Where are those thoughts? Who is responsible for them?

150
00:13:11,130 --> 00:13:11,338
[md]: You.

151
00:13:12,305 --> 00:13:12,850
[nm]: So, you are.

152
00:13:12,957 --> 00:13:13,137
[md]: You.

153
00:13:13,590 --> 00:13:13,936
[md]: Fully.

154
00:13:14,002 --> 00:13:15,850
[nm]: So, what's the citta's relationship to that?

155
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:25,539
[md]: The pressure to think those thoughts.

156
00:13:25,568 --> 00:13:26,040
[nm]: Exactly.

157
00:13:27,643 --> 00:13:31,080
[nm]: So, the dog is pressuring you, but you don't have to think that.

158
00:13:32,630 --> 00:13:43,212
[nm]: That's why in that whole, like, Sabbāsava Sutta about, well, all the taints, when it comes to these... No, sorry, that's not Sabbāsava Sutta.

159
00:13:43,270 --> 00:13:45,296
[nm]: Is it the one about, you know, the master of thought?

160
00:13:45,534 --> 00:13:46,157
[nm]: Which Sutta is that?

161
00:13:47,386 --> 00:13:49,042
[md]: Yeah, 19, 20.

162
00:13:49,452 --> 00:13:50,770
[nm]: Oh, what was the title, yeah?

163
00:13:52,490 --> 00:13:58,189
[md]: Stilling of thoughts, or something like that... But, let's go on and maybe I can...

164
00:13:58,190 --> 00:14:02,116
[nm]: Well, yeah, there's all those points of like refining your thoughts, thinking the opposite thoughts, you know.

165
00:14:02,111 --> 00:14:02,850
[md]: Yeah, that's...

166
00:14:02,860 --> 00:14:04,269
[nm]: See, that's what I keep saying to people.

167
00:14:05,386 --> 00:14:11,709
[nm]: If you're doing that because you're trying to get rid of the thoughts, means you're not even seeing your mind to begin with.

168
00:14:12,552 --> 00:14:17,694
[nm]: You're blaming the thoughts for the problem and then trying to get rid of them by, you know, sensuality: think asubha.

169
00:14:18,150 --> 00:14:21,027
[nm]: Hatred: think love, or something.

170
00:14:21,480 --> 00:14:22,836
[nm]: Like, you're not in your mind.

171
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,107
[nm]: You're doing it because you're blaming the thoughts.

172
00:14:25,716 --> 00:14:28,664
[nm]: So, first, you have to not try to get rid of the thoughts.

173
00:14:29,700 --> 00:14:32,521
[nm]: That's the first message you give to the pressuring dog.

174
00:14:34,535 --> 00:14:34,938
[nm]: No.

175
00:14:35,381 --> 00:14:38,990
[nm]: See, it barks at you, because it also thinks that the thoughts are the problem.

176
00:14:39,534 --> 00:14:41,083
[nm]: Thoughts are scaring the animal.

177
00:14:41,619 --> 00:14:43,585
[nm]: So, the animal is barking at the thoughts.

178
00:14:44,717 --> 00:14:46,518
[nm]: And if you go along with it, well,

179
00:14:46,686 --> 00:14:49,204
[nm]: You're affirming what the animal is craving against.

180
00:14:51,740 --> 00:14:56,552
[nm]: So, by conveying the message of: no, thoughts are not the problem,

181
00:14:56,586 --> 00:15:03,027
[nm]: I will endure them, so that the animal can see some space between its own rage and the thoughts.

182
00:15:04,070 --> 00:15:05,878
[nm]: But the animal is not thinking the thoughts.

183
00:15:06,118 --> 00:15:06,656
[nm]: You are.

184
00:15:09,156 --> 00:15:15,187
[nm]: So, that's why then, by not trying to get rid of the thoughts, by enduring the pain of the mind,

185
00:15:15,536 --> 00:15:25,796
[nm]: you also get to see where the pain comes from, in case you didn't have that view clarified beforehand, which is the pain comes from the animal that pressures and craves, not the thoughts.

186
00:15:27,117 --> 00:15:33,437
[nm]: So then, why would you think asubha instead of sensuality?

187
00:15:33,847 --> 00:15:35,629
[nm]: What would be the right reason to do so?

188
00:15:39,350 --> 00:15:54,147
[md]: Teach the mind how to not present that... how to... to teach the mind... how to see things right, basically?

189
00:15:54,203 --> 00:15:55,161
[nm]: Yes, yeah.

190
00:15:55,221 --> 00:15:57,566
[nm]: To basically... to... to calm it down.

191
00:15:58,048 --> 00:16:01,621
[nm]: So, the mind is excited in regard to this thing...

192
00:16:02,050 --> 00:16:05,355
[nm]: well, in regard to the same thing, you show it the ugly aspects.

193
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:06,918
[nm]: You know, I say.

194
00:16:07,340 --> 00:16:11,044
[nm]: The dog wants to have what's in the bag, and it's really losing its mind over it.

195
00:16:11,624 --> 00:16:16,787
[nm]: Well, you show it what's in the bag, and it's in the bag, and what's in the bag is something absolutely disgusting that it doesn't want to eat.

196
00:16:17,233 --> 00:16:18,736
[nm]: And then, you're like, okay, no, sorry.

197
00:16:19,050 --> 00:16:21,391
[nm]: And it will walk away and lose interest in it.

198
00:16:21,398 --> 00:16:22,613
[nm]: So, that's why you're doing it.

199
00:16:23,352 --> 00:16:28,067
[nm]: So, all those methods, quote-unquote, of countering the thoughts, you're not countering the thoughts.

200
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:37,209
[nm]: You're just broadening, basically, the context of,e perspective, exactly, of the very thoughts, so the animal doesn't go in the direction that it wants to go.

201
00:16:38,046 --> 00:16:38,955
[nm]: Same with hatred.

202
00:16:41,738 --> 00:16:44,945
[nm]: Because it's the mind of metta.

203
00:16:45,432 --> 00:16:46,201
[nm]: Not you.

204
00:16:46,223 --> 00:16:47,246
[nm]: You are not thinking metta.

205
00:16:47,253 --> 00:16:54,281
[nm]: You want mind... You want the dog to be in the state of unperturbed friendliness.

206
00:16:57,442 --> 00:17:06,290
[nm]: So, if you have, like, thoughts of ill-will, pressured towards thoughts of ill-will by the animal, well, you broaden the context.

207
00:17:06,715 --> 00:17:15,413
[nm]: Show it the thoughts of the peril of ill-will, the unsatisfactoriness, the limitations, and then the mind will be like, oh, yeah, it's not really worth it.

208
00:17:15,881 --> 00:17:17,197
[nm]: So, the mind will drop it.

209
00:17:17,233 --> 00:17:18,310
[nm]: You don't need to drop anything.

210
00:17:20,720 --> 00:17:24,533
[md]: So, is it like... I was just... I was trying to kind of think how to put it.

211
00:17:24,680 --> 00:17:31,951
[md]: Would be the... Would be a way of describing how to teach the mind in a general sense.

212
00:17:34,058 --> 00:17:37,949
[md]: Because what the mind wants, when it craves, is peace, basically.

213
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:39,796
[md]: What it's after is peace.

214
00:17:40,060 --> 00:17:43,556
[md]: But it is going about it the wrong way.

215
00:17:43,849 --> 00:17:44,126
[nm]: Yeah.

216
00:17:44,135 --> 00:17:45,219
[md]: Because that's all it knows.

217
00:17:45,233 --> 00:17:45,820
[nm]: Exactly.

218
00:17:46,077 --> 00:17:51,180
[md]: And so, what you need to know first is where peace actually comes from.

219
00:17:51,236 --> 00:17:53,180
[nm]: Yeah. You need to learn that yourself.

220
00:17:53,277 --> 00:17:56,242
[md]: So, you need to understand where the peace comes from.

221
00:17:56,259 --> 00:17:56,620
[nm]: Yeah.

222
00:17:56,760 --> 00:18:08,717
[md]: And then, like, you can use, basically, any of these specific things, like asubha and, like, in regard to sensuality, or any of them, would pertain to where the peace actually is.

223
00:18:08,976 --> 00:18:09,270
[sd]: Yeah.

224
00:18:09,374 --> 00:18:20,395
[md]: Like, kind of teaching the mind in, okay, in this instance, the peace is not in the direction of sensuality, the peace is actually in the direction of unsensuality, of disgust.

225
00:18:20,395 --> 00:18:26,528
[nm]: Well, see, if you answer the question I was going to ask, that's basically what the treat is.

226
00:18:26,596 --> 00:18:27,551
[nm]: That's the reward.

227
00:18:28,920 --> 00:18:32,904
[nm]: You can't give it sensuality as a reward, because as I said, that will just agitate it further.

228
00:18:33,430 --> 00:18:36,882
[nm]: But fundamentally, exactly, even citta wants peace.

229
00:18:37,490 --> 00:18:42,960
[nm]: That's why it pressures towards resolution of sensual desire, or ill-will, or any hindrance.

230
00:18:43,653 --> 00:18:46,596
[nm]: So, if you learn what the actual peace is, i.e.

231
00:18:46,620 --> 00:19:02,286
[nm]: what that right balance is, which you would have learned through simply not trying to get rid of thoughts, or blaming them, or blaming external circumstances, and being able to endure things on the right level, then, when the animal, say... say, is agitated,

232
00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:05,306
[nm]: You presented the the other side of the same thing.

233
00:19:05,706 --> 00:19:08,340
[nm]: It calms down and that's where you show it.

234
00:19:08,349 --> 00:19:09,280
[nm]: Look, there is a reward.

235
00:19:09,284 --> 00:19:09,840
[md]: You tell it. Yeah.

236
00:19:10,280 --> 00:19:10,841
[nm]: You tell it.

237
00:19:10,927 --> 00:19:11,948
[nm]: That's a reward.

238
00:19:11,951 --> 00:19:15,941
[nm]: You do the... you do the, basically, the audible sign,

239
00:19:16,351 --> 00:19:20,920
[nm]: sound with with what happens, so it starts to associate too, like the dog.

240
00:19:21,534 --> 00:19:22,509
[nm]: you use the clicker.

241
00:19:23,432 --> 00:19:26,067
[md]: Because like initially, that's the thing, it won't even...

242
00:19:26,065 --> 00:19:31,183
[nm]: It won't necessarily see, and it won't know where to stop, because you don't know where the peace is.

243
00:19:31,192 --> 00:19:32,836
[nm]: You don't know what's actually peaceful.

244
00:19:33,774 --> 00:19:38,638
[nm]: But if you do know what's peaceful, you'll see when the animal is within that peace, and then you can tell it.

245
00:19:39,641 --> 00:19:43,510
[nm]: And you do that sufficient amount of time, and it will start to see for itself.

246
00:19:44,810 --> 00:19:46,191
[nm]: Then, you don't need a clicker anymore.

247
00:19:47,631 --> 00:19:51,250
[nm]: Because, fundamentally, yes, exactly, it doesn't want to crave.

248
00:19:53,910 --> 00:19:54,530
[md]: And I once...

249
00:19:54,710 --> 00:20:01,100
[md]: that I was thinking that's like what you related... it relates to what you said as well about the arahant, when he knows it's liberated.

250
00:20:05,675 --> 00:20:13,216
[md]: Like... Because when you're restraining and everything,

251
00:20:14,100 --> 00:20:21,973
[md]: It, eh... you know, from one point of view, you're putting like a... you're kind of chaining it down and tying it down.

252
00:20:21,995 --> 00:20:22,339
[md]: Yeah.

253
00:20:22,393 --> 00:20:27,397
[md]: But really, kind of fundamentally, it's the animal that's chained from the start.

254
00:20:27,930 --> 00:20:33,600
[md]: Like, it's kind of chained in regard to... well, because it craves, can't help craving.

255
00:20:33,786 --> 00:20:34,174
[nm]: Hmm.

256
00:20:34,329 --> 00:20:37,187
[md]: So, it's kind of... it's not liberated in that sense.

257
00:20:37,185 --> 00:20:37,968
[nm]: It's bothered.

258
00:20:38,002 --> 00:20:38,600
[nm]: I wouldn't use...

259
00:20:38,620 --> 00:20:40,276
[nm]: I wouldn't say it was already chained.

260
00:20:40,341 --> 00:20:40,579
[nm]: No.

261
00:20:40,572 --> 00:20:43,098
[nm]: You chain it through... through precepts and sense restrain.

262
00:20:43,103 --> 00:20:43,850
[nm]: You fence it off.

263
00:20:44,465 --> 00:20:46,460
[nm]: But it's already agitated.

264
00:20:47,060 --> 00:20:53,423
[nm]: Like the wild, the wild elephant, the wild dog, whatever, whatever animal you want to use for this contemplation.

265
00:20:54,698 --> 00:20:55,258
[nm]: It's wild.

266
00:20:55,369 --> 00:20:56,795
[nm]: And that's what it means, basically:

267
00:20:56,860 --> 00:20:58,290
[nm]: impulses are controlling it.

268
00:20:58,930 --> 00:20:59,941
[nm]: It acts out of craving.

269
00:21:00,492 --> 00:21:02,130
[nm]: It avoids pain.

270
00:21:02,695 --> 00:21:03,624
[nm]: It seeks pleasure.

271
00:21:04,324 --> 00:21:06,552
[nm]: And it's absolutely oblivious to neutral feeling.

272
00:21:08,344 --> 00:21:08,819
[nm]: So...

273
00:21:11,413 --> 00:21:13,980
[nm]: It would be fine if it were not your problem.

274
00:21:14,653 --> 00:21:17,595
[nm]: But the point is, whatever that animal does, you're the one who's going to feel it.

275
00:21:19,696 --> 00:21:20,666
[nm]: So, it's like, oh...

276
00:21:20,933 --> 00:21:25,300
[nm]: So, then, it is in my best interest, and animal's best interest, to train it.

277
00:21:26,940 --> 00:21:28,598
[nm]: To teach it how to not crave.

278
00:21:29,236 --> 00:21:36,430
[nm]: And that's why the whole, like... Well, obviously, that's why, like, nothing... nothing can match the pleasure of liberation.

279
00:21:37,452 --> 00:21:43,737
[nm]: Or, like, sotāpatti, anāgāmī, arahant, because it doesn't come... it's not like...

280
00:21:44,310 --> 00:21:48,843
[nm]: You engage with the senses and then you get like a sort of pleasant feedback.

281
00:21:49,903 --> 00:21:52,436
[nm]: It's like completely not your own doing.

282
00:21:52,923 --> 00:21:57,660
[nm]: It's the animal that stops craving, but obviously you fully understand that it's entirely your own doing.

283
00:21:58,482 --> 00:22:12,780
[nm]: Because no wild animal, by any stretch, by any realm, can sort of calm itself down, in a sense to remove the craving, remove the impulse, and acting out of it.

284
00:22:12,820 --> 00:22:13,713
[nm]: That's inconceivable.

285
00:22:14,121 --> 00:22:15,517
[nm]: Because it's self-perpetuating.

286
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:17,522
[nm]: Anyway, pick an animal in the world.

287
00:22:18,100 --> 00:22:19,495
[nm]: Doesn't matter if it looks peaceful,

288
00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:26,766
[nm]: internally, every animal, what's in  common to that such animal realm in general, it has to act out of impulse.

289
00:22:28,174 --> 00:22:35,890
[nm]: Which means it has not enough capacity for that reflection where it can understand its own doing, its own input.

290
00:22:36,278 --> 00:22:37,330
[nm]: So, you teach it.

291
00:22:39,190 --> 00:22:42,998
[nm]: And now, it's completely... liberated.

292
00:22:44,712 --> 00:22:55,629
[nm]: Which means now, it's completely impossible for you, by any stretch, to experience even possibility of suffering.

293
00:22:57,517 --> 00:23:00,070
[nm]: Because the sole reason for suffering was the animal craving.

294
00:23:00,450 --> 00:23:00,816
[nm]: That's it.

295
00:23:02,419 --> 00:23:08,700
[nm]: And even more, obviously, as the suttas say, it will be impossible for you to be tied to another animal ever again.

296
00:23:22,351 --> 00:23:23,437
[nm]: So, that's how you reward it.

297
00:23:23,447 --> 00:23:31,560
[nm]: You reward it by thinking thoughts of the factual peace at the right time.

298
00:23:32,894 --> 00:23:39,449
[nm]: Not... you can be thinking them blindly as a method, but as with the dog training, the timing is important.

299
00:23:40,230 --> 00:23:42,252
[nm]: That's where you make an association.

300
00:23:44,950 --> 00:23:48,722
[nd]: Could you also say that it's the teaching

301
00:23:50,208 --> 00:23:55,551
[nd]: the value of neutral feeling only when neutral feeling arises.

302
00:23:55,589 --> 00:23:57,630
[nm]: No, I'm not, obviously, pre-empting it.

303
00:23:57,699 --> 00:23:59,271
[nm]: Yes. Yeah. Well, that's exactly.

304
00:23:59,275 --> 00:24:01,563
[nm]: That's what you... you're setting up a neutral feeling.

305
00:24:03,248 --> 00:24:04,250
[nd]: And then, clicker comes.

306
00:24:04,270 --> 00:24:06,334
[nm]: Through de-agitating the animal, yes.

307
00:24:06,350 --> 00:24:07,444
[nm]: Through calming it down.

308
00:24:07,470 --> 00:24:15,243
[nm]: Through... through, basically, not acting out of the pressure that the animal is putting on you.

309
00:24:16,562 --> 00:24:19,481
[nm]: And then, exactly, when there is a neutral feeling established,

310
00:24:21,233 --> 00:24:23,600
[nm]: you show that factual recognition.

311
00:24:24,142 --> 00:24:24,642
[nm]: You think it.

312
00:24:25,220 --> 00:24:26,247
[nm]: You recognize it.

313
00:24:26,916 --> 00:24:36,259
[nm]: Because, obviously, anything you see, smell, taste, touch, and think, is what animal experiences.

314
00:24:38,557 --> 00:24:41,100
[nm]: So, that's where your responsibility comes in.

315
00:24:41,750 --> 00:24:46,392
[nm]: Oh, you know, if everything is already anatta, then nobody kills, nobody... No.

316
00:24:46,475 --> 00:24:47,250
[nm]: You are doing it.

317
00:24:49,626 --> 00:25:03,343
[nm]: So, you choose sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, thoughts, which means you choose to keep the animal wild or tame it on account of following suitable sight, sound, smell, taste, touches or unsuitable, as the sutta says.

318
00:25:06,157 --> 00:25:17,832
[nm]: So, by thinking factually thoughts of restraint, relinquishment, withdrawal from unwholesome, the benefit of that...

319
00:25:18,860 --> 00:25:20,882
[nm]: you are putting those things in front of an animal.

320
00:25:20,955 --> 00:25:22,820
[nm]: So, you are familiarizing it with it.

321
00:25:25,170 --> 00:25:31,088
[nm]: So, it will... it will get to see it. It will get to learn it. It will get to associate it for itself.

322
00:25:31,951 --> 00:25:33,520
[nm]: And that's when you know your work is done.

323
00:25:33,850 --> 00:25:35,267
[nm]: When you don't have to keep putting it.

324
00:25:35,570 --> 00:25:44,002
[nm]: Now, the animal also prefers non-craving, non-agitation, and so on.

325
00:25:44,218 --> 00:25:45,910
[nm]: And then it's like, okay, my work's done now.

326
00:25:56,581 --> 00:26:03,240
[nd]: There's a tendency to ignore the neutral feeling.

327
00:26:03,597 --> 00:26:11,420
[nd]: It undermines that reward, like the ability to... I mean... becoming indolent towards...

328
00:26:12,507 --> 00:26:14,048
[nm]: But that's... whose tendency is that?

329
00:26:19,309 --> 00:26:20,069
[nm]: Yours or animals?

330
00:26:20,529 --> 00:26:20,809
[nm]: Mine.

331
00:26:21,520 --> 00:26:21,895
[nm]: Exactly.

332
00:26:23,870 --> 00:26:24,130
[nm]: So.

333
00:26:25,365 --> 00:26:26,066
[nm]: Stop ignoring it.

334
00:26:26,339 --> 00:26:26,623
[nd]: Yeah.

335
00:26:30,340 --> 00:26:36,453
[nm]: And that's it: you could understand now, on the basis of what we described, how, like, was it...

336
00:26:37,770 --> 00:26:39,495
[nm]: It was Gotamī, wasn't it? It was his mother.

337
00:26:40,690 --> 00:26:50,453
[nm]: When she came and asked for a brief instruction to go into the forest for the vassa, he says, whatever you know it's unsuitable, unwholesome, don't do it, basically.

338
00:26:50,700 --> 00:26:51,646
[nm]: Don't engage with it.

339
00:26:51,660 --> 00:26:53,413
[nm]: Whatever you know it's wholesome, engage with it.

340
00:26:55,030 --> 00:27:10,305
[nm]: It's like, if you stick to that, on the level of body-speech-mind, i.e: sight-sound-smell-taste-touches-thoughts, well, it will be highly unlikely, virtually impossible, that the animal is not tamed.

341
00:27:11,520 --> 00:27:19,549
[nm]: Because, see, it's wilderness, it needs fuel, it needs maintenance, it needs perpetuation.

342
00:27:19,780 --> 00:27:25,837
[nm]: So, and you're doing it, through carelessly attending to unsuitable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, thoughts.

343
00:27:27,903 --> 00:27:44,455
[nm]: So, if you stop carelessly attending to those things, and whenever those things pop up out of your own mental habits, well, you don't lose the context, you don't lose yoniso manasikāra, you broaden the context by presenting the bigger picture, and then the animal starts to learn.

344
00:27:44,458 --> 00:27:46,130
[nm]: Oh, whenever it's this, it's this.

345
00:27:47,077 --> 00:27:48,698
[nm]: Thus, it calms down.

346
00:27:49,132 --> 00:27:51,536
[nm]: It stops craving, and you will know when it stops craving.

347
00:27:52,933 --> 00:27:54,613
[nm]: You will know when the dog listens to you.

348
00:27:54,910 --> 00:27:58,921
[nm]: Like, you don't need to... hmm, how does this happen now? Let me psychologize about it.

349
00:28:06,660 --> 00:28:11,158
[nm]: That's also... mind you, this is not really relevant, but it's kind of interesting, I suppose.

350
00:28:12,727 --> 00:28:18,130
[nm]: Like in the suttas, when... when, say, all the asavas is abandoned, the animal is tamed,

351
00:28:19,170 --> 00:28:22,896
[nm]: there are no more, kind of, underlying tendencies there.

352
00:28:25,122 --> 00:28:36,670
[nm]: When, it says, like, if he were to direct his mind towards the memory of the previous lives, and if there is an opening, he will remember it.

353
00:28:38,770 --> 00:28:40,110
[nm]: He's not doing the remembering.

354
00:28:40,673 --> 00:28:41,665
[nm]: You are not doing that.

355
00:28:42,690 --> 00:28:44,356
[nm]: You just tell your mind to go and fetch.

356
00:28:46,210 --> 00:28:47,112
[md]: It's the same with any memory.

357
00:28:47,117 --> 00:28:55,796
[nm]: And the dog just goes and runs, and you wait, and it comes back after, and it fetched some memorabilia that now you remember.

358
00:28:58,029 --> 00:28:58,450
[nm]: Exactly.

359
00:28:58,509 --> 00:28:59,789
[nm]: It's the same with any memory.

360
00:29:00,235 --> 00:29:07,806
[nm]: So, people already deep down can have a hint of the animal that's not... they're doing, so to speak.

361
00:29:08,155 --> 00:29:08,955
[nm]: How do you remember?

362
00:29:09,339 --> 00:29:11,160
[nd]: So, remembering, like, what I ate today?

363
00:29:12,199 --> 00:29:13,359
[nm]: Well, yeah, how do you remember it?

364
00:29:13,886 --> 00:29:17,083
[nm]: Try to remember something that you haven't already thought before you said it.

365
00:29:19,090 --> 00:29:19,849
[nm]: Try to remember something.

366
00:29:19,847 --> 00:29:20,700
[nm]: What I ate yesterday.

367
00:29:21,520 --> 00:29:24,305
[nm]: What did you do ten years ago?

368
00:29:24,364 --> 00:29:24,984
[nm]: Just remember it.

369
00:29:25,184 --> 00:29:25,444
[nm]: Yeah.

370
00:29:26,820 --> 00:29:32,007
[nm]: Did you come up with the memory or did you just sort of ten years ago and then the memory was presented to you?

371
00:29:33,700 --> 00:29:34,242
[nd]: Yeah, exactly.

372
00:29:34,964 --> 00:29:35,540
[nm]: Every single time.

373
00:29:35,762 --> 00:29:38,901
[nm]: Especially when you can't remember something and you're trying.

374
00:29:39,190 --> 00:29:42,031
[nm]: That's when you realize how much it's not your doing.

375
00:29:44,816 --> 00:29:54,550
[nm]: And then, if you try too hard, that becomes a thing now, that's obstructing the animal from actually, and then there's no [??], I have to untry a bit, and then you remember.

376
00:29:54,707 --> 00:29:57,970
[nm]: In other words, then, the animal remembers it for you.

377
00:30:02,273 --> 00:30:06,002
[nm]: So, who is that you I keep talking about?

378
00:30:07,927 --> 00:30:10,802
[nm]: So, we have determined the animal is what citta is.

379
00:30:14,094 --> 00:30:19,550
[nm]: Who is this you that is responsible for what you feed to the animal, what you present it with?

380
00:30:28,300 --> 00:30:29,238
[nd]: It's a dialogue.

381
00:30:30,174 --> 00:30:33,107
[nm]: Well, yes, but how would a sutta refer to it?

382
00:30:33,582 --> 00:30:35,183
[nm]: What would a sutta call that?

383
00:30:45,460 --> 00:30:46,351
[md]: The individual...

384
00:30:47,408 --> 00:30:52,467
[nm]: Individual would be exactly... it would be like the collection, say, of all of it.

385
00:30:52,470 --> 00:30:53,932
[nm]: Sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, thoughts.

386
00:30:54,419 --> 00:31:01,081
[nm]: But the specific sense of you, I am, your point of view, what is that?

387
00:31:01,726 --> 00:31:08,201
[nm]: In terms of the sutta classifications and terminology, what would that... what would the closest thing to that be?

388
00:31:09,860 --> 00:31:11,197
[sd]: Five clinging aggregates?

389
00:31:17,355 --> 00:31:18,121
[nm]: More specific.

390
00:31:20,800 --> 00:31:21,200
[nd]: Mano?

391
00:31:21,846 --> 00:31:22,066
[nm]: Yeah.

392
00:31:23,595 --> 00:31:24,360
[nm]: You were guessing, yeah?

393
00:31:24,492 --> 00:31:25,861
[nd]: That was the first thing that came to mind.

394
00:31:26,031 --> 00:31:26,920
[nm]: It is mano.

395
00:31:26,923 --> 00:31:28,530
[nm]: Because it's the intellect, yeah.

396
00:31:29,040 --> 00:31:37,573
[nm]: That's exactly why Dhammapada sutta... Dhammapada verse says that mano is the forerunner of everything unwholesome, not citta.

397
00:31:39,983 --> 00:31:51,270
[nm]: Because you realize, well, yes, sure, wholesome and wholesome craving comes from citta on account of what mano has been feeding it, what mano has been thinking, what images it has been revolving around.

398
00:31:52,335 --> 00:31:56,197
[nm]: That is what's going to reflect back onto the animal and that's what's going to be looking at.

399
00:32:00,591 --> 00:32:01,590
[nm]: So, there you go.

400
00:32:02,210 --> 00:32:05,263
[nm]: Finally, the difference between citta and māno has been cleared.

401
00:32:08,014 --> 00:32:11,122
[nm]: So, once and for all, yes...

402
00:32:11,864 --> 00:32:15,970
[nm]: So, let's now say...

403
00:32:16,024 --> 00:32:16,960
[nm]: So, where is the animal?

404
00:32:21,219 --> 00:32:25,136
[nm]: In your own experience, how do you... Because, see, you can't think the animal.

405
00:32:26,002 --> 00:32:29,680
[nm]: Like, you think your citta, but that's obviously images which is mano.

406
00:32:37,476 --> 00:32:41,687
[tn]: So, how do you relate?

407
00:32:42,370 --> 00:32:44,441
[nm]: How do you reveal?

408
00:32:45,110 --> 00:32:50,516
[nm]: How do you connect with with the animal?

409
00:32:50,903 --> 00:32:51,623
[nm]: Where do you look for it?

410
00:32:52,606 --> 00:32:53,000
[nd]: Mood.

411
00:32:53,620 --> 00:32:53,866
[nm]: Hmm?

412
00:32:54,009 --> 00:32:54,340
[nd]: Mood.

413
00:32:56,518 --> 00:32:59,764
[nm]: Okay, but see, the mood is something you're perceiving.

414
00:33:02,830 --> 00:33:07,035
[md]: Being aware of the general attitude

415
00:33:07,471 --> 00:33:10,996
[md]: In regards to whatever you are attending to or doing.

416
00:33:12,049 --> 00:33:12,810
[nm]: And where would that be?

417
00:33:14,250 --> 00:33:16,892
[nm]: How would you be aware of the general attitude?

418
00:33:21,970 --> 00:33:25,692
[md]: By reminding yourself of it, bringing it to mind.

419
00:33:27,010 --> 00:33:29,461
[nm]: But the attitude, would that be something you're holding?

420
00:33:31,410 --> 00:33:39,120
[md]: No, that's the... that's why I'm saying it would be how you would relate to the citta, because it's something that is on the level of citta.

421
00:33:39,884 --> 00:33:40,485
[nm]: The attitude.

422
00:33:41,321 --> 00:33:42,740
[nm]: Give me some examples of the attitude.

423
00:33:42,760 --> 00:33:44,506
[nd]: Any craving, any greed in...

424
00:33:44,780 --> 00:33:50,615
[nm]: Well, yes, theoretically, any craving, any greed is in citta, but I'm asking how do you really find that citta?

425
00:33:50,858 --> 00:33:52,511
[md]: Attitude of...

426
00:34:00,980 --> 00:34:04,259
[tn]: You're saying in the background, where do you find your mind?

427
00:34:04,360 --> 00:34:04,986
[nm]: Where is your mind?

428
00:34:07,653 --> 00:34:13,187
[nm]: So, what I'm asking is, say, somebody asks you, how do I find my mind?

429
00:34:13,680 --> 00:34:14,543
[nm]: How do I reveal it?

430
00:34:14,560 --> 00:34:18,429
[nm]: Because, obviously, if you think it, it's the thoughts you're thinking.

431
00:34:22,053 --> 00:34:23,987
[nm]: So, attitude, yes, but...

432
00:34:25,723 --> 00:34:26,063
[nm]: Attitude.

433
00:34:27,592 --> 00:34:28,969
[nm]: How do I contemplate attitude?

434
00:34:30,250 --> 00:34:32,349
[nm]: How do I reveal my mind on account of an attitude?

435
00:34:34,160 --> 00:34:36,058
[nm]: I think... Is there a more direct way?

436
00:34:36,121 --> 00:34:37,021
[nm]: You know, that's what I'm...

437
00:34:37,041 --> 00:34:37,241
[sd]: Okay.

438
00:34:40,353 --> 00:34:43,878
[sd]: Where the kind of discomfort is, in a way.

439
00:34:43,900 --> 00:34:47,980
[sd]: Not like... Obviously, that needs some clarification, but...

440
00:34:48,000 --> 00:34:49,158
[nm]: So, where the feeling is?

441
00:34:49,625 --> 00:34:49,905
[sd]: Yeah.

442
00:34:50,260 --> 00:34:51,231
[sd]: So, what's felt?

443
00:34:57,352 --> 00:34:59,042
[nm]: Obviously, the mind is not the feeling.

444
00:35:01,830 --> 00:35:03,551
[nm]: But it can't be found apart from it.

445
00:35:05,006 --> 00:35:13,229
[nm]: So, how do you then get to, even more directly, to that can't be found apart from feeling, on account of feeling?

446
00:35:23,173 --> 00:35:24,193
[nd]: Physicality.

447
00:35:25,777 --> 00:35:27,497
[nm]: Yeah, in a way, yeah.

448
00:35:30,884 --> 00:35:31,667
[nm]: What does

449
00:35:32,096 --> 00:35:34,547
[nm]: What we refer to as physicality, i.e.

450
00:35:36,050 --> 00:35:42,581
[nm]: mindfulness of your form internally, what does that do?

451
00:35:47,956 --> 00:35:49,953
[nm]: Again, in regard to revealing your mind.

452
00:35:49,973 --> 00:35:50,739
[nd]: There's a contrast between...

453
00:35:53,170 --> 00:35:54,390
[nm]: You could say that way, I suppose.

454
00:35:59,435 --> 00:36:00,416
[nm]: So then, if you have

455
00:36:00,890 --> 00:36:22,429
[nm]: Say, if you have physicality, like mindfulness of the body internally, mindfulness of that physicality internally, and feeling, felt, not just thought about, where's the citta there?

456
00:36:27,471 --> 00:36:27,910
[nd]: Between.

457
00:36:31,306 --> 00:36:31,880
[nm]: Between what?

458
00:36:31,941 --> 00:36:33,231
[nd]: In that relationship.

459
00:36:34,766 --> 00:36:35,607
[nm]: Well, yeah, I mean...

460
00:36:36,228 --> 00:36:37,909
[nd]: The fact that there is a relationship.

461
00:36:38,969 --> 00:36:45,350
[nm]: Well, what, what, what, what does... What kind of relationship is that?

462
00:36:46,340 --> 00:36:49,209
[nd]: A... unfound...

463
00:36:49,216 --> 00:36:51,040
[nd]: I mean, theoretically, it's an unf...

464
00:36:51,144 --> 00:36:54,620
[nd]: I mean, it's a... one that requires interrogation.

465
00:36:54,642 --> 00:36:56,933
[nm]: Okay, but that's irrelevant then for where citta is.

466
00:36:58,730 --> 00:37:01,856
[nm]: So, what part of the relationship is relevant for which it is?

467
00:37:02,658 --> 00:37:16,990
[nm]: So, your physicality and your feeling can help you discern peripherally, because you can't think it directly, citta... on account of what you discern there.

468
00:37:21,369 --> 00:37:22,250
[nm]: So, don't think it.

469
00:37:23,095 --> 00:37:23,856
[nm]: Feel the feeling.

470
00:37:24,910 --> 00:37:26,436
[nm]: Experience the physicality.

471
00:37:32,430 --> 00:37:33,852
[md]: Are you referring to resistance?

472
00:37:34,421 --> 00:37:35,720
[nm]: No.

473
00:37:35,730 --> 00:37:38,560
[nm]: Well, I mean, it could be another approach, yeah.

474
00:37:39,103 --> 00:37:40,044
[nm]: But no, no, I'm not.

475
00:37:40,615 --> 00:37:42,763
[nm]: Say there's a neutral feeling, you're not resisting anything.

476
00:37:44,207 --> 00:37:44,987
[nm]: Where's the citta there?

477
00:37:47,723 --> 00:37:59,134
[nm]: So, like now, as we're talking about it, experiencing the physicality internally, feeling the feeling, what else is there?

478
00:38:01,374 --> 00:38:02,040
[nm]: Not theoretically.

479
00:38:02,060 --> 00:38:04,016
[nm]: What else is there specifically in your experience?

480
00:38:04,690 --> 00:38:05,561
[nm]: Is there something else?

481
00:38:06,717 --> 00:38:07,960
[nm]: What type of feeling is it?

482
00:38:10,807 --> 00:38:11,187
[nm]: Pleasant?

483
00:38:11,200 --> 00:38:11,815
[nm]: Unpleasant?

484
00:38:11,837 --> 00:38:12,240
[nm]: Neutral?

485
00:38:21,212 --> 00:38:21,833
[nm]: So...

486
00:38:21,835 --> 00:38:22,450
[tn]: The intention.

487
00:38:22,746 --> 00:38:23,050
[nm]: Sorry?

488
00:38:23,161 --> 00:38:23,633
[tn]: Intention.

489
00:38:23,781 --> 00:38:28,113
[nm]: Well, yes, it is intention, but I want people to come

490
00:38:29,040 --> 00:38:31,798
[nm]: to that conclusion, in their own description and the words.

491
00:38:33,682 --> 00:38:58,135
[nm]: So, if physicality reveals, or basically, experiencing physicality internally, sets you, resets you into your situation, the general experience as a whole, the framework of it, where you can't be outside of, the present moment, quote-unquote, feeling,

492
00:38:58,457 --> 00:38:59,077
[nm]: Being felt.

493
00:39:04,477 --> 00:39:05,495
[nm]: And then, what else?

494
00:39:05,982 --> 00:39:06,903
[nm]: What else are you having there?

495
00:39:06,920 --> 00:39:08,019
[nm]: Pressure towards...

496
00:39:08,390 --> 00:39:08,744
[nm]: Okay.

497
00:39:08,758 --> 00:39:10,000
[nm]: So, why is the pressure there?

498
00:39:11,800 --> 00:39:12,581
[nm]: Towards what?

499
00:39:13,456 --> 00:39:14,356
[nm]: On account of what?

500
00:39:21,478 --> 00:39:22,484
[md]: Well, there would be...

501
00:39:22,484 --> 00:39:24,121
[md]: If there's pressure, it would be

502
00:39:25,389 --> 00:39:34,417
[md]: To, like, let's say, intend something, do something, on account of a perception, something that you're... something that you're attending to, on account of...

503
00:39:34,637 --> 00:39:35,518
[md]: For example...

504
00:39:35,512 --> 00:39:35,810
[nm]: Okay.

505
00:39:35,869 --> 00:39:36,270
[md]: I don't know.

506
00:39:36,510 --> 00:39:38,962
[md]: He's asking me this question, I must give an answer.

507
00:39:39,393 --> 00:39:39,760
[nm]: Okay.

508
00:39:39,800 --> 00:39:40,346
[md]:  For example.

509
00:39:40,642 --> 00:39:42,356
[nm]: So, where is the citta there in that example?

510
00:39:42,960 --> 00:39:43,793
[nm]: You're still not telling me.

511
00:39:46,080 --> 00:39:46,600
[nd]: The pressure.

512
00:39:47,180 --> 00:39:47,640
[nm]: It's not.

513
00:39:48,189 --> 00:39:48,989
[nm]: That's not citta?

514
00:39:51,602 --> 00:39:51,950
[nm]: Sure.

515
00:39:52,212 --> 00:39:53,033
[nm]: It's closest to it.

516
00:39:53,664 --> 00:39:56,985
[nm]: But, I'm asking even more directly, accurately.

517
00:39:58,066 --> 00:40:01,927
[nm]: Like, for somebody who just started practicing, their mood is their citta...

518
00:40:02,360 --> 00:40:05,190
[nm]: they don't see anything else subtler than that.

519
00:40:06,440 --> 00:40:08,644
[nm]: But, obviously, the more you do it, it's not.

520
00:40:09,170 --> 00:40:10,509
[nm]: The pressure, it's not.

521
00:40:15,721 --> 00:40:17,936
[nm]: So, how do you see that which is invisible?

522
00:40:19,755 --> 00:40:20,715
[md]: Well, you have to see both.

523
00:40:21,110 --> 00:40:23,420
[md]: Like, if there's the feeling and the

524
00:40:23,760 --> 00:40:24,986
[md]: Let's say, if there's a pressure.

525
00:40:25,150 --> 00:40:25,464
[nm]: Yeah.

526
00:40:26,033 --> 00:40:26,414
[nm]: Okay.

527
00:40:26,470 --> 00:40:27,604
[md]: To intend.

528
00:40:28,077 --> 00:40:28,996
[nm]: So, you see both.

529
00:40:29,212 --> 00:40:29,791
[nm]: Exactly.

530
00:40:29,856 --> 00:40:30,324
[nm]: And?

531
00:40:31,903 --> 00:40:39,600
[md]: And then, seeing kind of... Well, the citta is basically like the space, quote-unquote, in between the two.

532
00:40:39,900 --> 00:40:40,940
[md]: That's how I put it.

533
00:40:41,123 --> 00:40:41,323
[nm]: Yeah.

534
00:40:41,544 --> 00:40:43,224
[nm]: And it doesn't need quote-unquote.

535
00:40:43,860 --> 00:40:49,832
[nm]: That is the space, the space created through neither here nor there.

536
00:40:51,953 --> 00:40:53,536
[nm]: And the reason why that's important

537
00:40:54,341 --> 00:40:58,957
[nm]: It's because you can be seeing the two and then coming up to see the third.

538
00:40:59,961 --> 00:41:01,219
[nm]: And you're not going to see the third.

539
00:41:02,016 --> 00:41:03,464
[nm]: It's going to fall on one of the two.

540
00:41:05,003 --> 00:41:16,690
[nm]: So, in order to see citta, then, you need to undo maybe 50, 60, maybe 80 percent of your craving to see the citta, in order to see it.

541
00:41:17,709 --> 00:41:18,320
[nm]: It depends.

542
00:41:18,506 --> 00:41:20,511
[nm]: Depends how strongly you want to see citta.

543
00:41:23,044 --> 00:41:31,140
[nm]: It's the absolute, probably most accurate, cleanest yoniso manasikāra.

544
00:41:32,033 --> 00:41:41,200
[nm]: You can have principles of it here and there, but when it comes to see citta, that is the only... like, there is... anything coarser than that, cannot, will not reveal it.

545
00:41:43,103 --> 00:41:50,766
[nm]: It will reveal the hints of it, the signs of the mind, you know, the simile of the cook, again, beforehand, but the point is,

546
00:41:51,180 --> 00:41:57,726
[nm]: you want to be able to directly know where it is, whenever and wherever it is, in that sense.

547
00:41:58,656 --> 00:42:10,087
[nm]: So, that's when you're always, well, keeping an eye on the dog, which means now you can have even more accurate timing for your instructions, for your commands, for your clickers.

548
00:42:14,785 --> 00:42:19,616
[nm]: So, basically, in order to see citta, you need to see both physicality and feeling,

549
00:42:19,970 --> 00:42:24,310
[nm]: And then probably endure that for a while, until your own craving to see the citta subsides.

550
00:42:27,975 --> 00:42:44,580
[nm]: And it would subside, because although you're not seeing the citta directly, by enduring the physicality, by experiencing the physicality with the currently enduring feeling, that is a message you're conveying to the citta that will basically calm it down, even if you're not seeing it.

551
00:42:46,732 --> 00:42:47,400
[nm]: And then you see it.

552
00:43:03,464 --> 00:43:06,753
[sd]: Can we speak a little bit more about the physicality?

553
00:43:07,090 --> 00:43:11,430
[sd]: I think I've been overthinking that these past few days.

554
00:43:15,672 --> 00:43:17,040
[nm]: So, what's your body posture now?

555
00:43:18,087 --> 00:43:18,520
[sd]: Sitting.

556
00:43:21,357 --> 00:43:23,100
[nm]: You didn't have to stand up to tell me that.

557
00:43:23,960 --> 00:43:24,150
[sd]: No.

558
00:43:24,979 --> 00:43:31,163
[nm]: Is there an internal... internally felt fact?

559
00:43:32,590 --> 00:43:33,784
[nm]: Of that seated body.

560
00:43:37,220 --> 00:43:37,524
[sd]: Yeah.

561
00:43:39,266 --> 00:43:40,967
[sd]: There is the fact that I'm sitting.

562
00:43:41,620 --> 00:43:42,404
[nm]: But internally.

563
00:43:42,698 --> 00:43:44,060
[nm]: An internal sense of it.

564
00:43:44,557 --> 00:43:46,317
[nm]: Like , I mean, on the inside.

565
00:43:46,348 --> 00:43:46,808
[nm]: Let's say that.

566
00:43:46,888 --> 00:43:47,528
[nm]: On the inside.

567
00:43:47,852 --> 00:43:48,240
[sd]: Right.

568
00:43:48,271 --> 00:43:50,792
[sd]: But I mean, when that fact that I'm sitting...

569
00:43:51,280 --> 00:43:51,640
[nm]: Sorry?

570
00:43:51,770 --> 00:43:53,653
[sd]: Like, the fact that I'm sitting with all my...

571
00:43:53,658 --> 00:43:54,800
[nm]: It's not the fact that you're sitting.

572
00:43:55,808 --> 00:43:59,294
[nm]: It's the internal fact of your sitting.

573
00:44:03,932 --> 00:44:10,580
[nm]: Or, the internal fact that's revealed through recognizing the fact of your external sitting.

574
00:44:10,800 --> 00:44:11,561
[nm]: Let's put it that way.

575
00:44:14,582 --> 00:44:15,242
[nm]: So, you're seated.

576
00:44:15,410 --> 00:44:16,727
[nm]: You cannot possibly doubt that.

577
00:44:17,883 --> 00:44:20,244
[nm]: Maybe you find a different word for it.

578
00:44:20,504 --> 00:44:21,884
[nm]: You could doubt how to express it.

579
00:44:23,300 --> 00:44:24,717
[nm]: But that fact you can't doubt.

580
00:44:27,506 --> 00:44:28,286
[nm]: But how do you know that?

581
00:44:30,160 --> 00:44:32,807
[nm]: Can you tell me you're seated, even if you close your eyes?

582
00:44:34,933 --> 00:44:37,520
[nm]: Even if you're not looking externally at your seated body.

583
00:44:40,145 --> 00:44:41,380
[nm]: So, what are you telling me then?

584
00:44:41,400 --> 00:44:46,625
[nm]: What are you... what are you seeing when you close your eyes and I say, what's your body posture?

585
00:44:47,500 --> 00:44:50,918
[nm]: How do you... Well, let me ask you this.

586
00:44:50,967 --> 00:44:54,516
[nm]: Close your eyes and I'll ask you, do you have a body?

587
00:44:55,515 --> 00:44:56,230
[nm]: Yes.

588
00:44:56,778 --> 00:44:57,590
[nm]: How do you know that?

589
00:44:58,547 --> 00:44:59,850
[nm]: Where did that knowledge come from?

590
00:45:05,738 --> 00:45:06,783
[sd]: It's just there.

591
00:45:07,120 --> 00:45:12,627
[sd]: It's just something that I remember... well...

592
00:45:13,847 --> 00:45:14,720
[nm]: You remembered it?

593
00:45:15,800 --> 00:45:17,936
[nm]: Where was it before you were remembering it?

594
00:45:18,200 --> 00:45:18,918
[nm]: Was it elsewhere?

595
00:45:23,398 --> 00:45:24,644
[nm]: Can it be elsewhere?

596
00:45:24,970 --> 00:45:27,233
[nm]: Can it be anywhere else but right there?

597
00:45:33,883 --> 00:45:34,463
[sd]: No.

598
00:45:35,008 --> 00:45:35,648
[nm]: Impossible.

599
00:45:35,963 --> 00:45:37,248
[nm]: You can be everywhere else.

600
00:45:37,540 --> 00:45:40,477
[nm]: You can forget about it, distract yourself, go to great extents.

601
00:45:40,940 --> 00:45:44,160
[nm]: But whenever you remember it, you find it where it always was.

602
00:45:45,250 --> 00:45:47,505
[nm]: That's the physicality, the internal side of that.

603
00:45:49,127 --> 00:45:53,456
[nm]: Not your associated images with it, which, of course, are going to follow.

604
00:45:54,150 --> 00:45:56,336
[nm]: When you think of your body, you're going to have images of that.

605
00:45:56,375 --> 00:46:00,021
[nm]: But, as I said, even the general question, do you have a body?

606
00:46:01,758 --> 00:46:02,999
[nm]: You already know the answer to that.

607
00:46:03,905 --> 00:46:05,886
[nm]: So, do I have a body?

608
00:46:07,275 --> 00:46:10,843
[nm]: And then, look at that internal fact of that thing there.

609
00:46:12,038 --> 00:46:19,561
[nm]: What we described in two talks ago, when we lost one, you know, the internal gravity, or whatever you want to call it.

610
00:46:20,356 --> 00:46:21,350
[nm]: There is a physicality.

611
00:46:21,391 --> 00:46:22,411
[nm]: That is the situation.

612
00:46:22,430 --> 00:46:29,973
[nm]: That is the framework for every other subsequent experience you might have on account of your senses.

613
00:46:35,390 --> 00:46:38,770
[sd]: It's kind of the being embodied, so to speak.

614
00:46:40,101 --> 00:46:40,620
[nm]: Sure.

615
00:46:40,630 --> 00:46:41,629
[nm]: Yeah, you can say that way.

616
00:46:42,848 --> 00:46:51,280
[nm]: So the point of this is that it's not to, like, externally observe your body, but to discern that internal one.

617
00:46:52,463 --> 00:46:55,289
[nm]: And that's the whole satipaṭṭhāna of body within the body.

618
00:46:55,849 --> 00:47:00,410
[nm]: You start thinking the body and find the body, experience the body within your thoughts of that body.

619
00:47:01,230 --> 00:47:08,981
[nm]: And when that becomes your thoughts of the body, experience further internal body in regard to those thoughts of body, to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

620
00:47:09,153 --> 00:47:17,219
[sd]: I think that's kind of what I was... like, just that... the tendency of kind of getting the idea of, like, oh, it's a mental image... You will.

621
00:47:17,216 --> 00:47:17,940
[sd]: You will. That's how it works.

622
00:47:18,140 --> 00:47:20,989
[nm]: So... Everything has to basically be an image like that.

623
00:47:21,206 --> 00:47:26,570
[nm]: But the point is, through discerning them rightly, you won't take them for granted.

624
00:47:26,770 --> 00:47:27,150
[nm]: That's it.

625
00:47:27,553 --> 00:47:30,346
[nm]: Because there is the factual physicality...

626
00:47:32,080 --> 00:47:41,866
[nm]: always at that same place, so to speak, at that same space, that you are experiencing.

627
00:47:45,140 --> 00:47:52,295
[nm]: So, in that sense, I know it's not obviously accurate, strictly speaking, but yes, you can feel that, as opposed to think it.

628
00:47:52,960 --> 00:47:57,432
[nd]: But, yeah, like you were saying earlier, that feeling is always experienced.

629
00:47:58,269 --> 00:47:58,490
[nm]: What?

630
00:47:58,830 --> 00:48:03,396
[nd]: So, yeah, yeah, I'm just basically repeating what I was... what you just said, in the sense that...

631
00:48:03,406 --> 00:48:05,510
[nm]: Ah, that feeling, you mean the body is always experienced?

632
00:48:05,512 --> 00:48:06,290
[nm]: Yeah, of course, yeah.

633
00:48:06,310 --> 00:48:09,784
[nm]: That experience of that internal body is always there.

634
00:48:09,873 --> 00:48:10,190
[nd]: And...

635
00:48:10,930 --> 00:48:11,983
[nm]: It's just being covered up.

636
00:48:12,087 --> 00:48:14,380
[nd]: Regardless of the mood, regardless of the pressure.

637
00:48:14,426 --> 00:48:15,873
[nm]: Regardless, regardless, regardless.

638
00:48:15,870 --> 00:48:17,665
[nm]: It's the... it's... it's the container.

639
00:48:17,980 --> 00:48:18,727
[nm]: Let's put it that way.

640
00:48:19,120 --> 00:48:19,580
[nd]: So that...

641
00:48:20,041 --> 00:48:25,379
[nm]: It's the basis for all of your, as I said, particular sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, thoughts.

642
00:48:27,220 --> 00:48:27,992
[nd]: That... that would be...

643
00:48:29,200 --> 00:48:37,081
[nd]: Like, taking the pressure, for example, as a sign of the... as the mind, but then, not... that... that...

644
00:48:37,990 --> 00:48:39,076
[nm]: There, we're taking the pressure.

645
00:48:39,090 --> 00:48:40,317
[nm]: Sorry, yes.

646
00:48:40,935 --> 00:48:41,597
[nm]: It's alright.

647
00:48:41,810 --> 00:48:57,619
[nd]: The... the uninterruptedness of feeling, feeling, will be, yeah, less perturbed, the more that, yeah, this practice of... of...

648
00:49:00,450 --> 00:49:03,231
[nd]: discerning the relationship between physicality of the body and...really.

649
00:49:03,280 --> 00:49:10,683
[nd]: Well, yeah, but see, like, you're not... you're only discerning the relationship marginally, really.

650
00:49:10,736 --> 00:49:11,800
[nm]: It's not about the relationship.

651
00:49:12,271 --> 00:49:17,032
[nm]: It's not about discerning it in a sense of figuring it out, how it relates.

652
00:49:17,476 --> 00:49:19,425
[nm]: It relates by being there.

653
00:49:19,735 --> 00:49:20,894
[nm]: These two things are there.

654
00:49:20,900 --> 00:49:21,752
[nm]: That's the relationship.

655
00:49:21,757 --> 00:49:29,660
[nm]: So, yes, you do need to discern that, because for as long as you think it relates, it comes from here into there, and then it goes elsewhere, yes, of course...

656
00:49:30,200 --> 00:49:31,250
[nm]: You're not going to see any of it.

657
00:49:31,961 --> 00:49:44,525
[nm]: But if you're recognizing the just mere juxtaposition of the internal physicality and feeling felt, that's the relationship you want to...

658
00:49:44,540 --> 00:49:49,609
[nm]: But... not... well, of course, discern and then really not cover up.

659
00:49:52,860 --> 00:49:53,820
[nd]: Even if it's neutral.

660
00:49:54,610 --> 00:49:55,141
[nm]: Especially.

661
00:49:55,183 --> 00:49:56,067
[nd]: Yeah, exactly.

662
00:49:56,960 --> 00:49:57,755
[nm]: That's the whole point.

663
00:49:58,627 --> 00:50:03,216
[nm]: So, yes, you could say there's physicality and there's neither pleasure nor pain felt.

664
00:50:03,390 --> 00:50:03,801
[nm]: Great.

665
00:50:05,069 --> 00:50:08,969
[nm]: So, don't cover up that experience now.

666
00:50:14,690 --> 00:50:16,174
[nm]: And that's, you know, what I was saying before.

667
00:50:16,190 --> 00:50:18,007
[nm]: And then, if you have thoughts: ok, but where's my citta?

668
00:50:18,332 --> 00:50:21,023
[nm]: Include that in the experience of those two things.

669
00:50:22,200 --> 00:50:24,380
[nm]: Don't give into it and try to resolve it.

670
00:50:25,500 --> 00:50:28,652
[nm]: You're closer to resolution by not acting out of that craving...

671
00:50:29,360 --> 00:50:30,290
[nm]: than by acting out of it.

672
00:50:31,163 --> 00:50:33,306
[nm]: But, of course, it was their very craving that brought you there.

673
00:50:34,487 --> 00:50:50,974
[nm]: If you didn't crave to see the citta, if you were not pressured towards it, if you did not generate a desire to do so, well, you would have not have... you would have not discerned the relationship between the internal physicality and feeling.

674
00:51:06,840 --> 00:51:12,530
[nm]: So, it's kind of like... you know how the aggregate of virtue sets up a fence around the wild animal?

675
00:51:13,610 --> 00:51:21,229
[nm]: Well, this is clearly like a... another sort of... layer to it.

676
00:51:22,637 --> 00:51:33,110
[nm]: So, you might not know, at times, where my mind is, or discern it peripherally correctly, but you know physicality and the feeling.

677
00:51:33,209 --> 00:51:35,163
[nm]: So, you know what has to be within that space.

678
00:51:36,810 --> 00:51:38,305
[nm]: That's why the space was relevant.

679
00:51:39,410 --> 00:51:40,921
[nm]: Cannot be outside of this.

680
00:51:41,088 --> 00:51:41,726
[nm]: Impossible.

681
00:51:41,870 --> 00:51:57,425
[nm]: In the same sense, well, I don't know where my moods come from, and what they are, and how they act, and how they pressure me, but I know they cannot pressure me on account of household life, they cannot pressure me on account of sensuality, ill-will, of this coarse nature in terms of the precepts.

682
00:51:57,780 --> 00:51:59,961
[nm]: They cannot, because those things have been abandoned.

683
00:52:14,315 --> 00:52:15,975
[tn]: Back to this physicality.

684
00:52:17,410 --> 00:52:24,763
[tn]: If you look at Majjhima 62, you see, like, the Buddha teaching Rahula,

685
00:52:26,099 --> 00:52:27,936
[tn]: and before he gets to anapanasati,

686
00:52:29,294 --> 00:52:33,408
[tn]: he's talking about the five aggregates are not yours.

687
00:52:33,418 --> 00:52:34,816
[tn]: So, that's what you must see them as.

688
00:52:35,258 --> 00:52:41,718
[tn]: Then, he goes to the elements, but with specific bodily contemplation.

689
00:52:41,713 --> 00:52:56,598
[tn]: So, it's like whatever is internal, that is hard and solid, weight, gravity, and taken up, that is the earth element.

690
00:52:57,522 --> 00:52:58,007
[tn]: And

691
00:52:58,448 --> 00:53:00,184
[tn]: And it's like the hair of the head.

692
00:53:02,720 --> 00:53:04,465
[tn]: You have that... it gives you that idea.

693
00:53:04,499 --> 00:53:06,232
[tn]: Hair of the head, that thought.

694
00:53:06,300 --> 00:53:09,864
[tn]: Hair of the body, nails, teeth.

695
00:53:10,523 --> 00:53:20,043
[tn]: And it's like, to me, it's... you've got to find that internal, hard, solid, corresponding aspect.

696
00:53:20,510 --> 00:53:22,087
[tn]: You have an idea of hair of the head.

697
00:53:25,940 --> 00:53:28,240
[tn]: Where's the corresponding hardness of it?

698
00:53:28,256 --> 00:53:29,951
[tn]: Is that... Where's the hardness?

699
00:53:30,700 --> 00:53:33,006
[tn]: Where's the solidity of the weight of it?

700
00:53:34,510 --> 00:53:40,758
[tn]: Is it... You can't... You know, you can have the idea of the word and the concept...

701
00:53:40,800 --> 00:53:46,130
[nm]: Yeah, what is the internal solidity of it?

702
00:53:46,290 --> 00:53:49,110
[nm]: Not external, because somebody could be touching the hair or something.

703
00:53:50,370 --> 00:53:51,003
[nm]: No, internally.

704
00:53:51,677 --> 00:54:03,990
[nm]: So, you want to include all those images in that internal solidity... And why is that important?

705
00:54:05,420 --> 00:54:14,800
[nm]: Now, while we're talking about it... Why is it important to say... see all the five aggregates as not mine?

706
00:54:14,918 --> 00:54:21,852
[nm]: Or all the form, internal, external, earth, water, fire, air, to leave nothing out?

707
00:54:22,290 --> 00:54:23,098
[nm]: Why is that important?

708
00:54:23,098 --> 00:54:26,219
[nm]: To include it in that relationship of physicality, felt.

709
00:54:35,730 --> 00:54:39,507
[md]: Whatever is left out is taken up as mind, isn't it?

710
00:54:40,640 --> 00:54:45,023
[nm]: But in terms of what we've been talking about, citta, why is it relevant for that?

711
00:54:46,305 --> 00:54:50,740
[nm]: Yes, of course, it's taken as mind, but in terms of this whole talk,

712
00:54:52,330 --> 00:54:53,803
[nm]: why is it relevant to include everything?

713
00:54:54,170 --> 00:54:56,467
[sd]: Wait, to include it all within the relationship?

714
00:54:56,838 --> 00:54:57,284
[sd]: Or...

715
00:54:57,452 --> 00:54:57,818
[tn]: Yes.

716
00:54:58,310 --> 00:55:03,546
[nm]: Well, the relationship establishes, as I said, and then whatever is left out, you realize cannot be left out.

717
00:55:03,600 --> 00:55:10,880
[nm]: So, then, you are intentionally unleaving it out by bringing it up on the base of physicality felt.

718
00:55:12,254 --> 00:55:13,480
[nm]: Actually, that's a relationship, isn't it?

719
00:55:13,680 --> 00:55:14,715
[nm]: Physicality felt.

720
00:55:16,223 --> 00:55:19,258
[nm]: I don't need to say experience physicality and feel the feeling

721
00:55:19,912 --> 00:55:21,687
[nm]: Just physicality felt.

722
00:55:22,508 --> 00:55:23,389
[nm]: Both are already there.

723
00:55:25,180 --> 00:55:32,535
[nm]: So, why is it important to include things that were tacitly excluded?

724
00:55:33,250 --> 00:55:33,997
[sd]: What is that?

725
00:55:35,049 --> 00:55:37,010
[sd]: Is it that that's important?

726
00:55:37,018 --> 00:55:42,867
[sd]: Or just kind of if things are left out, then you're not seeing either end properly?

727
00:55:44,894 --> 00:55:47,083
[sd]: Well, so to speak, like... That is important.

728
00:55:51,675 --> 00:55:54,084
[sd]: But, I guess, this is...

729
00:55:54,720 --> 00:55:59,423
[nm]: So, you are seeing, say, you are factually experiencing physicality, feeling felt.

730
00:56:01,040 --> 00:56:07,718
[nm]: But, the Sutra encourages to start including all these seemingly less relevant things.

731
00:56:09,243 --> 00:56:13,854
[nm]: Oh, internal bile and bones and this... Why?

732
00:56:15,110 --> 00:56:17,120
[md]: Well, to disassociate those things.

733
00:56:17,236 --> 00:56:17,650
[nm]: From what?

734
00:56:17,670 --> 00:56:19,401
[md]: From the internal physicality.

735
00:56:23,280 --> 00:56:23,721
[nm]: Well, no.

736
00:56:23,729 --> 00:56:26,212
[nm]: You are kind of associating with internal physicality.

737
00:56:27,456 --> 00:56:28,341
[tn]: First, at least.

738
00:56:28,514 --> 00:56:29,154
[tn]: That's what you're doing.

739
00:56:30,290 --> 00:56:33,350
[nm]: They are disassociated from physicality internally: that's why they're left out.

740
00:56:34,730 --> 00:56:39,100
[nm]: When you think internal physicality, you don't have the images of your bones and marrow and stuff.

741
00:56:40,900 --> 00:56:42,254
[nm]: You haven't associated them with it.

742
00:56:43,878 --> 00:56:44,080
[md]: Okay.

743
00:56:44,082 --> 00:56:45,798
[md]: I'm going from the other direction.

744
00:56:47,520 --> 00:56:49,895
[nm]: So, in the context of what we've been talking about,

745
00:56:50,310 --> 00:57:00,829
[nm]: Mind, citta, the animal, why is it important to include all these images, all these things, all these implicit...

746
00:57:02,041 --> 00:57:07,052
[tn]:  Why is it important to include the image with the physicality, essentially?

747
00:57:07,070 --> 00:57:08,753
[nm]: Yeah, like what you were describing,

748
00:57:08,760 --> 00:57:15,570
[nm]: like, oh, any, okay, internally... body experienced internally and starts including...

749
00:57:16,030 --> 00:57:20,184
[nm]: the hair, the nails, the extent of all of that internally.

750
00:57:20,190 --> 00:57:21,386
[nm]: Why is that relevant?

751
00:57:21,425 --> 00:57:22,383
[nd]: It's peaceful.

752
00:57:22,404 --> 00:57:23,507
[nm]: Well, let's say like this.

753
00:57:23,655 --> 00:57:28,024
[nm]: No, again, that doesn't bear any answer to citta that I'm asking.

754
00:57:28,969 --> 00:57:32,368
[nm]: So, what happens to citta if you're not including it?

755
00:57:39,420 --> 00:57:44,792
[nm]: So, if these things are not included, how does that affect...

756
00:57:46,368 --> 00:57:46,768
[nm]: citta?

757
00:57:49,527 --> 00:57:50,330
[tn]: It's, eh...

758
00:57:53,401 --> 00:57:58,758
[tn]: It's not, eh... expanded, or it's narrowed, or not liberate.

759
00:57:58,790 --> 00:57:59,830
[nm]: It's not seen.

760
00:58:00,363 --> 00:58:03,520
[nm]: To the extent the things are not included, to that extent you won't be seeing it.

761
00:58:06,261 --> 00:58:16,252
[nm]: So, you can think you're seeing it, but if the internal extent of that physicality is not brought up,

762
00:58:18,613 --> 00:58:23,076
[nm]: to the certain size, let's call it that way, you won't be seeing the mind.

763
00:58:26,831 --> 00:58:35,086
[nm]: Because mind is what's left when everything else is included of that space.

764
00:58:36,670 --> 00:58:44,470
[nm]: That's why contemplation of infinite space, if you are refined to perceive it, immediately outlines where the mind is.

765
00:58:44,783 --> 00:58:46,373
[nm]: Because it won't be anywhere in the space.

766
00:58:46,632 --> 00:58:51,320
[nm]: So, infinite space means any possible direction of space up...

767
00:58:51,340 --> 00:58:53,263
[nm]: it doesn't matter what axis you take,

768
00:58:54,996 --> 00:59:00,128
[nm]: if you're perceiving that on that level, you are clearly then outlining the mind for what it's not.

769
00:59:08,858 --> 00:59:14,351
[nm]: Obviously, as I said, for someone who just starts practicing, becoming aware of their own thoughts, including them,

770
00:59:14,741 --> 00:59:20,720
[nm]: as real phenomena, everything else not that, will be their citta.

771
00:59:21,840 --> 00:59:28,259
[nm]: But yeah, citta has obviously proliferated through your own... well, through your own lack of discernment.

772
00:59:28,300 --> 00:59:29,061
[nm]: That's the point.

773
00:59:29,469 --> 00:59:43,280
[nm]: And for as long as citta is not clearly outlined internally, through what it is not, there's going to still be an underlying tendency to fall into the assumption

774
00:59:44,220 --> 00:59:48,455
[nm]: that the pressure came from here, or there, or this, or that.

775
00:59:50,330 --> 00:59:51,200
[nm]: That's the connection.

776
00:59:51,878 --> 00:59:54,608
[nm]: By the way, citta can't enter your mano and your world.

777
00:59:55,379 --> 00:59:56,584
[nm]: It's... the animal is animal.

778
00:59:56,650 --> 00:59:57,214
[nm]: You are you.

779
00:59:58,430 --> 01:00:10,797
[nm]: But through not fully delineating that, not fully understanding, clearly, the difference in those two domains, one is invisible domain of the animal,

780
01:00:13,346 --> 01:00:24,936
[nm]: that's how you basically, yourself, assume, project, pressure coming out of the thoughts, or this, or that, that pertains to you, not the animal.

781
01:00:30,787 --> 01:00:32,147
[nm]: Like how we started this...

782
01:00:34,312 --> 01:00:35,166
[nm]: When you have

783
01:00:38,030 --> 01:00:45,168
[nm]: anger, your... the thoughts to think these... pressure to think these cruel thoughts, we say, oh, well, who thinks the thoughts?

784
01:00:45,180 --> 01:00:46,266
[nm]: Was it you or the animal?

785
01:00:46,969 --> 01:00:51,060
[nm]: Oh, no, it's me, due to the pressure exhibited by the animal.

786
01:00:52,744 --> 01:01:01,546
[nm]: So, there's going to be traces of that pressure left for as long as things are not sufficiently included into that physicality felt here and now.

787
01:01:04,593 --> 01:01:12,746
[nm]: And when they are, then the mind is, how the sutra says, you know, ābhassara and bright and infinite and pure and unobstructed.

788
01:01:13,454 --> 01:01:14,293
[nm]: Still is the animal.

789
01:01:15,161 --> 01:01:25,934
[nm]: Still it's going to disintegrate like everything else, so you're not holding any mystical views in regard to it, but it's factually clean, obedient, no more clickers, no more hints.

790
01:01:26,916 --> 01:01:31,219
[nm]: It just does the right thing on its own accord every single time.

791
01:01:35,435 --> 01:01:36,329
[nm]: Makes the right choice.

792
01:02:05,890 --> 01:02:18,833
[nm]: And how would you know, practically speaking, not from the arahant's point of view, how would you know, when on some days, like, you are not outlining your mind clearly enough?

793
01:02:21,941 --> 01:02:23,401
[nm]: What would be the first symptom of that?

794
01:02:27,330 --> 01:02:29,619
[nm]: and it would remain throughout, that symptom.

795
01:02:29,658 --> 01:02:30,509
[nm]: I t wouldn't disappear.

796
01:02:39,522 --> 01:02:42,213
[nm]: Hint, we spoke about it in the last two talks.

797
01:02:45,173 --> 01:02:45,670
[tn]: Suffering.

798
01:02:46,938 --> 01:02:49,680
[nm]: Yeah, of course, but more practically.

799
01:02:51,200 --> 01:02:52,000
[md]: Resistance.

800
01:02:52,036 --> 01:02:52,916
[nm]: Resistance, yeah.

801
01:02:54,649 --> 01:02:55,912
[nm]: Of course, suffering, yes.

802
01:02:56,160 --> 01:02:59,718
[nm]: But, you know, okay, so I suffered as I resist.

803
01:03:01,709 --> 01:03:02,240
[tn]: Fighting.

804
01:03:03,309 --> 01:03:04,858
[nm]: Well, fight comes out of it.

805
01:03:05,052 --> 01:03:06,480
[nm]: Fight comes out of resistance.

806
01:03:06,500 --> 01:03:09,435
[nm]: So, why is it important for you,

807
01:03:11,580 --> 01:03:14,681
[nm]: for your mano, to give up resistance.

808
01:03:15,760 --> 01:03:18,484
[nm]: A gain, in the context of citta, that we speak about.

809
01:03:22,900 --> 01:03:25,796
[tn]: Because it's not gonna calm it down. It's not...

810
01:03:25,975 --> 01:03:26,480
[nm]: Well, exactly.

811
01:03:26,580 --> 01:03:27,345
[nm]: You just punish.

812
01:03:27,340 --> 01:03:36,152
[nm]: Exactly. You can be telling it what you want, but if the dog sees that you are the one resisting it, well, then, of course, it will continue doing its own resisting and craving against it.

813
01:03:36,180 --> 01:03:38,906
[md]: Yeah. So you don't add more of the same, basically.

814
01:03:38,910 --> 01:03:39,621
[nm]: Yeah. Yeah.

815
01:03:40,286 --> 01:03:47,670
[nm]: So, you practicing the unresisting of resistance in regard to what's felt, is another way of talking to your mind.

816
01:03:49,915 --> 01:03:57,990
[nm]: However coarsely you might be doing that, you realize, oh, it's already conveying the same message that I might be doing, you know, in the future on a more subtler level.

817
01:03:58,018 --> 01:03:58,559
[nm]: It doesn't matter.

818
01:04:14,557 --> 01:04:19,095
[nm]: So, here and now, internal physicality felt unresisted.

819
01:04:23,150 --> 01:04:25,786
[nm]: Then, add few things to that mix,

820
01:04:28,010 --> 01:04:29,323
[nm]: push it into that framework.

821
01:04:29,575 --> 01:04:37,985
[nm]: Include those thoughts of this form and that form, and internal, and body, and skin, and bones, and then unresisted.

822
01:04:38,429 --> 01:04:40,555
[nm]: And then include more, and then unresisted.

823
01:04:42,770 --> 01:04:44,750
[nm]: Yeah, include more into the physicality.

824
01:04:46,790 --> 01:04:52,751
[nm]: Or, start contemplating impermanence, destruction, cessation, and unresisted.

825
01:04:54,065 --> 01:04:55,338
[nm]: That's also talking to your mind.

826
01:04:55,460 --> 01:04:57,527
[nm]: That's also telling the obvious to the mind.

827
01:04:59,987 --> 01:05:00,632
[nd]: Danger.

828
01:05:01,241 --> 01:05:02,220
[nm]: Danger, exactly.

829
01:05:02,240 --> 01:05:03,638
[nm]: Gratification, danger, escape.

830
01:05:06,661 --> 01:05:10,470
[nm]: It's really just presenting these rightly seen phenomena,

831
01:05:12,198 --> 01:05:15,357
[nm]: even if you don't see the mind, by having them there, the mind's seeing them.

832
01:05:15,770 --> 01:05:18,928
[nm]: You know: I don't know where the dog is,

833
01:05:19,050 --> 01:05:21,922
[nm]: I know it's within this fence, because fence is impenetrable,

834
01:05:23,100 --> 01:05:26,419
[nm]: I don't know where it is, but if I play these sounds, I know it will have to hear it.

835
01:05:27,638 --> 01:05:31,430
[sd]: It's like when you're kind of prodding the elephant with spears and swords and stuff.

836
01:05:31,450 --> 01:05:32,416
[nm]: In the suttas, yes.

837
01:05:32,450 --> 01:05:33,003
[sd]: Yeah, yeah.

838
01:05:33,030 --> 01:05:35,127
[nm]: To teach it how to not move even further.

839
01:05:35,656 --> 01:05:35,856
[sd]: Yeah.

840
01:05:49,180 --> 01:05:56,181
[md]: I was thinking that it could also be, like we were talking a couple of talks ago, how do you gladden the mind?

841
01:05:58,533 --> 01:06:13,343
[md]: And that actually, maybe it's somewhat less direct, but I think bringing up, like, for example, you know, the things, like think concrete things that represent impermanence, for example...

842
01:06:13,690 --> 01:06:15,493
[md]: and I'm unresisting that.

843
01:06:15,750 --> 01:06:18,989
[md]: It's kind of how to gladden it further, basically. It becomes...

844
01:06:19,610 --> 01:06:30,686
[nm]: Well, imagine, again, imagine like you're with a dog and there is something... there's a factually agitating sight, for both you and the dog.

845
01:06:31,347 --> 01:06:33,156
[nm]: And the dog is just barking and losing it.

846
01:06:33,580 --> 01:06:35,195
[nm]: So, you can't remove the sight.

847
01:06:36,031 --> 01:06:40,528
[nm]: You can't even tell to a dog that actually there is no danger, because it's already agitated.

848
01:06:40,955 --> 01:06:45,447
[nm]: So you just sort of, you know, pet it and show it, physically, that you are calm...

849
01:06:46,270 --> 01:06:47,735
[nm]: and then the dog will calm down.

850
01:06:48,172 --> 01:06:54,969
[nm]: All the thing is still there, and it still might be edgy about it, but it would calm down, because you unresisted yourself.

851
01:06:58,962 --> 01:07:04,414
[nm]: So, that was the relevance of, you know, what he's talking about in those previous two talks, was exactly that.

852
01:07:04,480 --> 01:07:09,995
[nm]: It's just... Yeah, you might know, you don't... you don't necessarily see the animal that clearly, or not always, and so on...

853
01:07:10,380 --> 01:07:15,735
[nm]: but factually, by unresisting yourself, again, as I said, the animal has to hear it, because you know it's there.

854
01:07:24,012 --> 01:07:27,803
[sd]: In one of the older videos, you were talking about

855
01:07:29,086 --> 01:07:30,033
[sd]: just this kind of...

856
01:07:31,310 --> 01:07:36,858
[sd]: I think it was in the context of this kind of overthinking, like, oh, what is the body?

857
01:07:37,056 --> 01:07:39,658
[sd]: You said it is just like the actual body there.

858
01:07:40,232 --> 01:07:40,637
[nm]: Hmm.

859
01:07:41,306 --> 01:07:41,895
[sd]: Uhm.

860
01:07:43,796 --> 01:07:45,818
[sd]: At least that's... that's how I remember it.

861
01:07:46,040 --> 01:07:52,865
[sd]: It does seem like that's kind of a pretty concrete way of just cutting through the... I mean, of course,

862
01:07:52,863 --> 01:07:53,800
[nm]: Yeah, well, whatever...

863
01:07:53,820 --> 01:07:56,072
[nm]: whatever sort of help a person needs.

864
01:07:56,072 --> 01:07:56,404
[sd]: Yeah.

865
01:07:56,560 --> 01:07:59,008
[nm]: But the point is to to look for it and you realize:

866
01:07:59,490 --> 01:08:03,820
[nm]: oh, I find it, not by finding it, but by uncovering it, because it's already there.

867
01:08:03,820 --> 01:08:06,009
[sd]: Right, you're not making it... so

868
01:08:06,160 --> 01:08:08,457
[tn]: [?Well within this], there's a sense of touch.

869
01:08:08,937 --> 01:08:09,826
[tn]: Like...

870
01:08:10,980 --> 01:08:11,430
[nm]: Well

871
01:08:11,819 --> 01:08:12,658
[tn]: you know...

872
01:08:13,840 --> 01:08:16,644
[nm]: Start with a sense of touch, but then you have to look past it.

873
01:08:16,946 --> 01:08:18,180
[tn]: That's the closest and then...

874
01:08:18,950 --> 01:08:21,104
[tn]: So, as you can see, like bodily sensations.

875
01:08:21,181 --> 01:08:22,141
[nm]: Sure, sure.

876
01:08:22,235 --> 01:08:23,236
[tn]: But that's the...

877
01:08:23,250 --> 01:08:25,964
[nm]: But really, it's not really closer to the internal body.

878
01:08:25,980 --> 01:08:27,922
[nm]: Internal body is the saḷāyatana.

879
01:08:28,108 --> 01:08:29,848
[tn]: Yeah, as sights are.

880
01:08:30,184 --> 01:08:31,531
[nm]: Yeah, exactly, exactly, yeah.

881
01:08:31,540 --> 01:08:32,131
[tn]: Smells.

882
01:08:32,206 --> 01:08:33,640
[nm]: Smells are already touching it.

883
01:08:33,888 --> 01:08:35,471
[nm]: Sounds are already landing on it.

884
01:08:36,677 --> 01:08:38,406
[nm]: It's not more closer.

885
01:08:38,608 --> 01:08:42,775
[nm]: It's just that people approach the body through the external images of it.

886
01:08:43,200 --> 01:08:50,520
[nm]: So then, see, what I think my body is, it's this thing here, and inside of that is these sensations and touches...

887
01:08:50,530 --> 01:08:52,277
[nm]: thus, that must be more of the body.

888
01:08:53,210 --> 01:08:55,582
[nm]: But that's already completely upside down.

889
01:08:56,126 --> 01:09:04,355
[nm]: Like, the whole idea of external, it's on the count of you already experiencing that physicality here and now, internally.

890
01:09:05,628 --> 01:09:10,735
[nm]: That's the framework for anything, whether it's external or then internal again.

891
01:09:17,354 --> 01:09:17,574
[md]: Yeah.

892
01:09:17,770 --> 01:09:32,671
[md]: I mean, it's something I was thinking a while ago, but, like, at some point, I remember having the thought or realizing it's kind of completely gratuitous to think that, like, through the eyes, what I'm perceiving is the room.

893
01:09:32,913 --> 01:09:33,542
[md]: And then,

894
01:09:34,188 --> 01:09:37,135
[md]: Like, I'm perceiving my body with the sense of touch.

895
01:09:37,145 --> 01:09:37,285
[nm]: Yeah.

896
01:09:37,426 --> 01:09:40,064
[md]: And it's like, I'm just as well perceiving the room with my body.

897
01:09:40,080 --> 01:09:40,920
[nm]: Yeah, exactly, yeah.

898
01:09:41,340 --> 01:09:48,813
[nm]: No, that's... I think we said that before. And I think even in some philosophies it was said, all you are perceiving is your senses perceiving things.

899
01:09:50,655 --> 01:09:58,013
[nm]: So, the space revealed with your eyes is secondary to your experience of eyes seeing space revealed objects.

900
01:10:01,460 --> 01:10:03,086
[nm]: But, yeah, the minds...

901
01:10:03,495 --> 01:10:04,256
[nm]: through the default...

902
01:10:04,410 --> 01:10:05,706
[nm]: desire, non-restraint.

903
01:10:07,170 --> 01:10:12,253
[nm]: Well, not even, as I said, not even recognizing thoughts and images to be real as such.

904
01:10:13,542 --> 01:10:15,024
[nm]: Not seeing them as phenomena.

905
01:10:15,480 --> 01:10:18,757
[nm]: That's how you then proliferate further into that space.

906
01:10:18,780 --> 01:10:25,617
[nm]: And then that space, externally, which you perceive, becomes a reference point back onto you, or on you, or externally.

907
01:10:25,620 --> 01:10:27,891
[nm]: And then we're all equal, filling out this space...

908
01:10:28,310 --> 01:10:31,284
[nm]: But all of that's secondarily proliferate experience.

909
01:10:31,310 --> 01:10:33,224
[nm]: What your experience is sights, sounds...

910
01:10:33,231 --> 01:10:43,448
[nm]: What you experience is eyes, ears, nose,  tongue, body, mind, perceiving, sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, thoughts.

911
01:10:44,044 --> 01:10:47,631
[nm]: You are not... you do not have access to the sense objects.

912
01:10:50,070 --> 01:10:58,666
[md]: So that's what I kind of meant earlier when I think... because I thought, when you were asking about, like, including the...

913
01:10:59,664 --> 01:11:02,490
[md]: So, I was wondering about clarifying this anyway, so that's why I'm...

914
01:11:04,460 --> 01:11:33,231
[md]: The specific, I don't know, organs, like, I don't know, the hair on the head, or the... because... because I think what I thought you were referring to was, let's say, for example, you kind of start with these kind of, like, vague ideas or perceptions, like, and they would be kind of tactile perceptions, like, say, a perception of feet, or of knees, or whatever.

915
01:11:38,326 --> 01:11:43,722
[md]: That kind of stand for the body.

916
01:11:45,453 --> 01:11:45,784
[nm]: Yeah.

917
01:11:47,364 --> 01:11:51,328
[md]: But, but they're not the body.

918
01:11:52,344 --> 01:11:54,211
[md]: But they're not the internal body.

919
01:11:54,357 --> 01:11:56,520
[nm]: If they stand for it, they will be there and then.

920
01:11:58,140 --> 01:12:00,211
[md]: So, that's what I mean, like, but that's...

921
01:12:05,110 --> 01:12:14,562
[md]: I don't know, it seems to me that they stand for it, basically, by not being kind of fully discerned, or not being clearly discerned as images.

922
01:12:14,577 --> 01:12:19,239
[md]: Because, like, if I look at them, basically, I'm kind of saying this practically.

923
01:12:19,237 --> 01:12:20,815
[md]: I'm not sort of... this is not...

924
01:12:21,190 --> 01:12:29,900
[md]: it's like, I realize, well, actually, it can't... it can only be as another level of an image... and

925
01:12:29,930 --> 01:12:31,185
[nm]: But then, there's something else.

926
01:12:31,200 --> 01:12:35,085
[md]: Actually, there is something else, like, behind it, or below it.

927
01:12:35,510 --> 01:12:47,437
[md]: So, I think, what I understood was, like, it is kind of further clarifying or discerning more of the...

928
01:12:47,437 --> 01:12:54,637
[nm]: Yes, and there is a need for that, but also, that in and of itself does not necessarily translate...

929
01:12:55,575 --> 01:13:08,580
[nm]: It certainly removes the obstructions and it probably is improving your communication with the mind, but even of itself does not necessarily result in what we've been talking about in this talk.

930
01:13:08,580 --> 01:13:12,150
[md]: Yeah, no, this is slightly tangential... I just...

931
01:13:12,180 --> 01:13:18,462
[nm]: But yes, of course, yes, like that whole kasina practice, as we said before, you could eventually...

932
01:13:19,282 --> 01:13:26,140
[nm]: have images of the great element of earth and planet and whatever, universe, mountains, stones, the properties of it.

933
01:13:26,210 --> 01:13:37,287
[nm]: You can have images of those properties associated with your internal form, with your internal body, if you would have diligently abandoned everything else inferior to it.

934
01:13:39,790 --> 01:13:48,390
[nm]: But, another more like a practical side of including the body organs here, that's basically, it is what occupies the space of the body here.

935
01:13:49,590 --> 01:13:50,705
[nm]: You just leave it out.

936
01:13:51,642 --> 01:13:56,075
[nm]: And you judge, as I said, the space of the body by the space of your perception, so to speak.

937
01:13:57,890 --> 01:14:02,940
[nm]: But if you recognize, well, the space of the body, space of the perception, space is there and it's fully occupied to the brim.

938
01:14:03,810 --> 01:14:11,887
[nm]: Sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, images of what constitutes sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, there is nothing in and out left in anymore.

939
01:14:39,690 --> 01:14:52,945
[nm]: So, where does... where would a citta... where does a citta correspond to, approximately, in terms of that space that your body occupies?

940
01:14:55,730 --> 01:14:57,565
[nm]: Where would you place your citta?

941
01:15:04,877 --> 01:15:05,555
[md]: At the heart.

942
01:15:08,370 --> 01:15:09,462
[nm]: Kind of in that area.

943
01:15:09,790 --> 01:15:12,150
[nm]: Not specifically to the left or something.

944
01:15:18,310 --> 01:15:18,685
[nm]: Why?

945
01:15:18,692 --> 01:15:20,330
[nm]: Well, who knows.

946
01:15:20,972 --> 01:15:21,730
[nm]: It's irrelevant.

947
01:15:35,570 --> 01:15:47,715
[tn]: That's why this whole contemplation of these specific images, like bones and skin and whatever, you're kind of lighting up, you know, you... Yeah, you're lighting up.

948
01:15:47,727 --> 01:15:48,835
[nm]: Yeah, you're lighting up.

949
01:15:48,840 --> 01:15:50,495
[tn]: You're lighting up the whole kāyā.

950
01:15:50,645 --> 01:15:50,917
[nm]: Yeah.

951
01:15:51,762 --> 01:15:55,560
[tn]: And it's like that first step of anapanasati, you know.

952
01:15:55,567 --> 01:15:56,770
[nm]: Yeah, yeah, of course, of course.

953
01:15:56,790 --> 01:15:57,977
[tn]: Experience the whole body.

954
01:15:58,635 --> 01:16:02,318
[nm]: But by lighting up the whole kāyā, you realize it's all included.

955
01:16:02,370 --> 01:16:04,670
[nm]: Thus citta is not there.

956
01:16:05,662 --> 01:16:09,490
[nm]: So, by including everything, that's how you outline what's not there, which is citta.

957
01:16:12,275 --> 01:16:22,050
[md]: Actually, sorry, I'm now going to be slightly jumping, but you said, eh... but that was something that also occurred to me, like the... you were asking, like, where would you place your citta?

958
01:16:22,070 --> 01:16:22,400
[nm]: Mmhm.

959
01:16:23,280 --> 01:16:27,502
[md]: And it would be kind of corresponding to the internal heart.

960
01:16:28,970 --> 01:16:35,285
[md]: But I was thinking that could also be, eh... what it's referring to with parimukkha,

961
01:16:35,517 --> 01:16:39,435
[md]: Or, I mean, even more, sort of, bringing the...

962
01:16:39,450 --> 01:16:42,420
[nm]: Well, yeah, I think there is an overlap there. Of course, yeah.

963
01:16:43,427 --> 01:16:45,942
[md]: Anyway, it was kind of understood.

964
01:16:53,900 --> 01:16:58,150
[nm]: But yes, there are certain convergence points.

965
01:16:59,120 --> 01:17:10,052
[nm]: Convergence points of your own... body in its entirely, that an untrainned citta would [?prefer it]

966
01:17:12,040 --> 01:17:26,585
[nm]: In other words, means of expression, means of gaining pleasure, means of acting out, and it would be, let's say, descending upon those parts, like losing perspective in regard to everything else.

967
01:17:32,632 --> 01:17:37,752
[nm]: It's like, you know, I don't know, like, violent people have that expression, like, oh, these hands have the mind of their own.

968
01:17:38,919 --> 01:17:45,982
[nm]: Like, when they get angry and start fighting, beating, and... as in, like, that, just, that, that's all they, that's all they see.

969
01:17:45,997 --> 01:17:51,072
[nm]: So, it's like, factually, your mind would have been probably descended on your limbs.

970
01:17:52,632 --> 01:17:57,872
[nm]: Or, sexuality-wise, your groin, yeah, speech, your mouth.

971
01:17:58,797 --> 01:18:00,218
[nm]: Things that are factually taken up.

972
01:18:00,237 --> 01:18:15,365
[nm]: So, by practicing un-taking up, un-resisting, from your point of view, not making citta to do it, you un-resisting those convergent points, sends a message to citta.

973
01:18:17,360 --> 01:18:21,710
[nm]: Because it can't make you double down, unless you choose to double down.

974
01:18:25,877 --> 01:18:28,065
[nm]: So, that's the stilling of activations.

975
01:18:29,597 --> 01:18:37,090
[nm]: Physical, verbal and mental... stilling of taking-ups that you're doing.

976
01:18:37,410 --> 01:18:43,410
[nm]: It's still going to be taking-up that the animal's doing, but, as I said, you don't have to worry about that.

977
01:18:43,422 --> 01:18:53,202
[nm]: If you untake-up everything to be taken-up, then the animal will not be able to remain holding for much longer.

